const TOPICS = ["DataFrame basics & inspection", "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "Sorting and top-N selection", "GroupBy aggregation", "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "Merging and joining DataFrames", "Handling missing and duplicate data", "String manipulation and regex extraction", "Date and time manipulation", "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "Simulation & probability", "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic"];
const LESSONS = {"DataFrame basics & inspection": "\n<p>A pandas <b>DataFrame</b> is a labeled 2D table. <code>.shape</code>, <code>.columns</code>, <code>.head()</code>, <code>.dtypes</code> and <code>.describe()</code> are the first moves in any exercise &mdash; confirming what you're actually working with before writing a single filter.</p>\n<p><code>.nunique()</code> and <code>.isna().sum()</code> give a fast read on cardinality and data quality across every column at once.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> an interviewer might open with, \"Before you touch this data, what would you check first?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>print(customers.shape)\nprint(customers.dtypes)\nprint(customers.isna().sum())</pre></div>\n<p>Result: customers is (60, 6), every column is an object except customer_id (int64), and signup_date has 2 missing values. That last number alone tells you not to trust a signup-based cohort split until you've handled those two rows.</p>\n<p>In a live coding round, spending 30 seconds inspecting the data before diving in signals good habits, not slowness.</p>\n", "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing": "\n<p>Boolean masks are the core of pandas filtering: <code>df[df.col == value]</code> or the equivalent <code>.loc[...]</code> form. Combine conditions with <code>&amp;</code> and <code>|</code> (not <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>), and wrap each condition in parentheses.</p>\n<p><code>.isin([...])</code> tests membership, <code>~mask</code> negates it, and <code>.between(a,b)</code> is inclusive on both ends by default.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Pull every Enterprise customer who came in through a paid channel.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>mask = (customers[&#x27;segment&#x27;] == &#x27;Enterprise&#x27;) &amp; (customers[&#x27;acquisition_channel&#x27;] == &#x27;Paid&#x27;)\nresult = customers[mask][[&#x27;customer_id&#x27;, &#x27;first_name&#x27;, &#x27;country&#x27;]]</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 3 rows &mdash; customers 30, 45 and 50. Note the parentheses around each condition and <code>&amp;</code> instead of <code>and</code>; without both, pandas raises an error or silently does the wrong thing.</p>\n<p>This is the pandas equivalent of a SQL WHERE clause, and it's the most common thing you'll type in any data wrangling exercise.</p>\n", "Sorting and top-N selection": "\n<p><code>.sort_values(col, ascending=...)</code> sorts a DataFrame; pass a list of columns for a multi-key sort with a matching list of ascending flags.</p>\n<p><code>.head(n)</code>/<code>.tail(n)</code> grab the first/last n rows after sorting, and <code>.iloc[a:b]</code> slices by position for ranges like \"6th through 10th\".</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"What are our three biggest single transactions this period, and who made them?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>result = transactions.sort_values(&#x27;amount&#x27;, ascending=False).head(3)[[&#x27;transaction_id&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;, &#x27;amount&#x27;]]</pre></div>\n<p>Result: transaction 32 (customer 19, 1276.70), transaction 115 (customer 53, 1247.62), transaction 113 (customer 53, 1212.17). Customer 53 shows up twice in the top 3 &mdash; worth a follow-up question about whether that's one whale account or a data issue.</p>\n<p>Top-N and bottom-N questions (biggest transactions, earliest signups) come up constantly when sizing an opportunity.</p>\n", "GroupBy aggregation": "\n<p><code>.groupby(col)</code> splits rows into groups; chain an aggregate like <code>.sum()</code>, <code>.mean()</code> or <code>.size()</code> to collapse each group to one row &mdash; the direct equivalent of SQL's GROUP BY.</p>\n<p>Group by a list of columns to slice across multiple dimensions at once, e.g. country and segment together.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Break total revenue down by customer segment.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>merged = transactions.merge(customers, on=&#x27;customer_id&#x27;)\nresult = merged.groupby(&#x27;segment&#x27;)[&#x27;amount&#x27;].sum().round(2)</pre></div>\n<p>Result: Consumer 17273.91, Enterprise 3603.60, SMB 8309.98. Merging first is the key step &mdash; segment lives on customers, not transactions, so there's nothing to group by until the two tables are joined.</p>\n<p>Nearly every metric you report &mdash; revenue, active users, average order value &mdash; starts life as a groupby.</p>\n", "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)": "\n<p><code>.agg(name=('col','func'))</code> lets you compute several differently-named aggregates in a single groupby call, which is cleaner than chaining separate calls.</p>\n<p>To filter on an aggregate result (pandas has no direct HAVING), aggregate first into a DataFrame, then apply a normal boolean mask to that result.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"For each customer, I want their transaction count and total spend in one table, named clearly.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>result = transactions.groupby(&#x27;customer_id&#x27;).agg(\n    n_txns=(&#x27;transaction_id&#x27;, &#x27;size&#x27;),\n    total_spend=(&#x27;amount&#x27;, &#x27;sum&#x27;),\n).round(2)</pre></div>\n<p>Result for customer 53: n_txns=6, total_spend=3732.23. Naming the output columns inline like this beats a plain <code>.sum()</code> because you often need count and sum side by side to say something meaningful (\"6 transactions averaging 622 each\").</p>\n<p>This two-step \"aggregate, then filter\" pattern is exactly how you isolate cohorts worth acting on: heavy spenders, high-frequency users, popular products.</p>\n", "Merging and joining DataFrames": "\n<p><code>df.merge(other, on='key', how='inner'|'left'|...)</code> is pandas' JOIN. The default is an inner join; <code>how='left'</code> keeps every row from the left side and fills NaN where there's no match.</p>\n<p>The classic use of a left merge is finding absence: merge with <code>indicator=True</code> and keep rows where <code>_merge=='left_only'</code> to find customers who never transacted.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"How many signed-up customers have never actually made a transaction?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>m = customers.merge(transactions, on=&#x27;customer_id&#x27;, how=&#x27;left&#x27;, indicator=True)\nnever = m[m[&#x27;_merge&#x27;] == &#x27;left_only&#x27;]\nresult = never[&#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 25 customers. <code>indicator=True</code> adds a <code>_merge</code> column that tags every row as 'both', 'left_only' or 'right_only', which is the cleanest way to isolate exactly this kind of \"exists in A but not B\" question.</p>\n<p>You'll merge customers, products, transactions and events constantly to assemble the picture behind any metric.</p>\n", "Handling missing and duplicate data": "\n<p><code>.isna()</code> flags missing values, <code>.fillna(value)</code> replaces them, and <code>.dropna()</code> removes rows containing any. <code>.duplicated()</code> flags exact-duplicate rows, and <code>.drop_duplicates(subset=..., keep='first')</code> removes them.</p>\n<p>Real datasets are never as clean as a SQL schema promises &mdash; this is where \"data wrangling\" actually happens.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Before you trust any total off this transactions table, is it actually clean?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>print(customers[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;].isna().sum())\nprint(transactions.duplicated().sum())\nclean_transactions = transactions.drop_duplicates()</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 2 customers (ids 4 and 8) have a missing signup_date, and there's 1 fully-duplicated transaction row. Summing amount before calling <code>.drop_duplicates()</code> would silently overstate revenue &mdash; exactly the kind of check interviewers are listening for unprompted.</p>\n<p>Interviewers watch for whether you check for nulls/dupes before trusting an aggregate, not just whether you know the method names.</p>\n", "String manipulation and regex extraction": "\n<p>The <code>.str</code> accessor exposes vectorized string ops: <code>.str.upper()</code>, <code>.str.contains()</code>, <code>.str.startswith()</code>, <code>.str.len()</code>, all applied element-wise without a Python loop.</p>\n<p><code>.str.extract(r'(pattern)')</code> pulls out a regex capture group, and <code>.str.contains(pattern, regex=True)</code> tests a full regex match.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"For an alphabetical outreach split, how many customers have first names starting A through M?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>mask = customers[&#x27;first_name&#x27;].str.contains(r&#x27;^[A-M]&#x27;, regex=True)\nresult = mask.sum()</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 39 of 60 customers. The <code>.str</code> accessor applies the regex to every row at once &mdash; no explicit loop needed, and the same pattern works for extracting substrings with <code>.str.extract(r'(...)')</code>.</p>\n<p>Cleaning and parsing messy text fields (names, free-text categories, ids embedded in strings) is a constant in real analyst work.</p>\n", "Date and time manipulation": "\n<p><code>pd.to_datetime(col)</code> converts text dates into real datetime64 values, unlocking the <code>.dt</code> accessor: <code>.dt.strftime('%Y-%m')</code>, <code>.dt.weekday</code>, and datetime arithmetic.</p>\n<p>Subtracting two datetime columns gives a Timedelta; <code>.dt.days</code> turns that into a plain integer day count.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"What month did customer 13 sign up in, so we can put them in the right cohort?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>c = customers.copy()\nc[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;] = pd.to_datetime(c[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;])\nc[&#x27;signup_month&#x27;] = c[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;].dt.strftime(&#x27;%Y-%m&#x27;)\nresult = c.loc[c.customer_id == 13, [&#x27;customer_id&#x27;, &#x27;signup_month&#x27;]]</pre></div>\n<p>Result: customer 13 signed up 2024-02-23, which truncates to signup_month '2024-02'. Converting to real datetimes first is what unlocks <code>.dt</code> &mdash; without <code>pd.to_datetime</code>, signup_date is just text and <code>.dt.strftime</code> would raise an error.</p>\n<p>Cohort windows, time-to-first-payment, and retention periods all rest on this pattern.</p>\n", "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)": "\n<p><code>np.where(cond, if_true, if_false)</code> is a vectorized if/else across a whole column &mdash; much faster than looping. <code>np.select([cond1,cond2], [val1,val2], default=...)</code> handles more than two branches.</p>\n<p><code>.apply(func, axis=1)</code> runs a Python function per row when vectorized ops aren't enough, at the cost of speed.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Tag every transaction as 'high' or 'low' value at a $200 cutoff, without writing a loop.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>t = transactions.copy()\nt[&#x27;tier&#x27;] = np.where(t[&#x27;amount&#x27;] &gt;= 200, &#x27;high&#x27;, &#x27;low&#x27;)\nresult = t[&#x27;tier&#x27;].value_counts()</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 89 'low' and 52 'high' transactions. <code>np.where</code> evaluates the whole column at once; the equivalent <code>.apply(lambda row: ..., axis=1)</code> would give the same answer but run row-by-row in plain Python, which is much slower at scale.</p>\n<p>Prefer np.where/np.select over apply whenever possible &mdash; it's the difference between a instant answer and a slow one on large data.</p>\n", "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation": "\n<p><code>pd.pivot_table(df, values=, index=, columns=, aggfunc=)</code> reshapes long data into a summary matrix &mdash; rows and columns become the two dimensions you're comparing.</p>\n<p><code>pd.crosstab(a, b)</code> is a shortcut for count-based cross-tabs, and accepts <code>normalize='index'</code> or <code>'columns'</code> for percentages instead of raw counts.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Show me revenue broken out by segment down the side and transaction status across the top.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>merged = transactions.merge(customers, on=&#x27;customer_id&#x27;)\nresult = pd.pivot_table(merged, values=&#x27;amount&#x27;, index=&#x27;segment&#x27;, columns=&#x27;status&#x27;, aggfunc=&#x27;sum&#x27;).round(2)</pre></div>\n<p>Result: Consumer/completed=15700.91, Consumer/failed=905.24, Consumer/refunded=667.76, and similar cells for Enterprise and SMB (Enterprise has no refunded rows, so that cell is NaN). This is the shape an exec deck actually wants &mdash; one row per segment, one column per status.</p>\n<p>This is how you build the classic \"metric by segment by period\" table that ends up in an executive deck.</p>\n", "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff": "\n<p><code>.cumsum()</code> gives a running total, <code>.shift(1)</code> looks at the previous row, and <code>.diff()</code> computes the change from the previous row directly &mdash; all within an optional <code>.groupby(...)</code> to reset per group.</p>\n<p><code>.expanding().mean()</code> gives a running average, and <code>.rolling(n).mean()</code> gives a moving average over a fixed window.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Show customer 13's running total spend over time, transaction by transaction.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>t13 = transactions[transactions.customer_id == 13].sort_values(&#x27;txn_date&#x27;).copy()\nt13[&#x27;running_total&#x27;] = t13[&#x27;amount&#x27;].cumsum()\nresult = t13[[&#x27;txn_date&#x27;, &#x27;amount&#x27;, &#x27;running_total&#x27;]]</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 251.79 &rarr; 317.35 &rarr; 683.30 &rarr; 723.93 &rarr; 813.50. Sorting by date first is essential &mdash; <code>.cumsum()</code> just accumulates rows top to bottom, so an unsorted DataFrame gives a running total in the wrong order.</p>\n<p>Running totals and period-over-period change are the language of growth reporting.</p>\n", "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)": "\n<p><code>.rank(method=..., ascending=...)</code> ranks values, optionally within a <code>.groupby(...)</code> for a per-group rank.</p>\n<p>The key pattern is <b>keeping the latest row per group</b>: sort descending by date (with a tiebreaker), then <code>.drop_duplicates(subset='group_col', keep='first')</code> keeps exactly one row per group.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"What was customer 13's most recent transaction?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>t13 = transactions[transactions.customer_id == 13].sort_values(&#x27;txn_date&#x27;, ascending=False)\nresult = t13.drop_duplicates(subset=&#x27;customer_id&#x27;, keep=&#x27;first&#x27;)</pre></div>\n<p>Result: transaction 16 on 2024-07-03 for 89.57. Sorting descending by date then keeping the first row per customer_id is the standard \"latest record per group\" pattern &mdash; it generalizes directly to \"latest address per user\" or \"most recent status per order\" problems.</p>\n<p>Deduplication and latest-state logic come up whenever transactional data has repeats.</p>\n", "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long": "\n<p><code>pd.melt(df, id_vars=..., var_name=, value_name=)</code> turns wide columns into tidy long rows. <code>.stack()</code>/<code>.unstack()</code> move a column level between the row index and column index.</p>\n<p><code>pd.concat([df1, df2])</code> stacks two DataFrames with the same columns on top of each other.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Take that segment-by-status revenue pivot and turn it back into one tidy row per combination.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>merged = transactions.merge(customers, on=&#x27;customer_id&#x27;)\nwide = pd.pivot_table(merged, values=&#x27;amount&#x27;, index=&#x27;segment&#x27;, columns=&#x27;status&#x27;, aggfunc=&#x27;sum&#x27;).round(2).reset_index()\nresult = wide.melt(id_vars=&#x27;segment&#x27;, var_name=&#x27;status&#x27;, value_name=&#x27;revenue&#x27;)</pre></div>\n<p>Result: rows like (Consumer, completed, 15700.91), (Enterprise, completed, 3459.85), (SMB, completed, 7438.71), continuing for failed and refunded. The pivot table was easier to read; this long form is what you'd feed back into a groupby or a plotting library.</p>\n<p>Knowing when data needs to be wide (for a pivot report) versus long (for grouping/plotting) is a routine judgment call in analysis work.</p>\n", "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas": "\n<p>A <b>cohort</b> groups customers by a shared start, usually signup month via <code>.dt.strftime('%Y-%m')</code>. Retention measures how many of each cohort are still active in a later period.</p>\n<p>The pattern: tag each customer with their cohort month and a target comparison month, build a set of (customer, active-month) pairs from the activity table, then check membership.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Of the customers who signed up in January 2024, how many were still transacting in February?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>c = customers.copy()\nc[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;] = pd.to_datetime(c[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;])\nc[&#x27;cohort&#x27;] = c[&#x27;signup_date&#x27;].dt.strftime(&#x27;%Y-%m&#x27;)\njan_cohort = c[c[&#x27;cohort&#x27;] == &#x27;2024-01&#x27;]\n\nt = transactions.copy()\nt[&#x27;txn_date&#x27;] = pd.to_datetime(t[&#x27;txn_date&#x27;])\nfeb_active = set(t.loc[t[&#x27;txn_date&#x27;].dt.strftime(&#x27;%Y-%m&#x27;) == &#x27;2024-02&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;])\n\nretained = jan_cohort[&#x27;customer_id&#x27;].isin(feb_active).sum()\nresult = retained / jan_cohort[&#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 9 customers signed up in January, 6 of them transacted again in February, so the retention rate is 6/9 &asymp; 0.67. Building <code>feb_active</code> as a set makes the <code>.isin()</code> membership check both fast and easy to read.</p>\n<p>Retention curves are one of the most requested consumer growth deliverables.</p>\n", "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas": "\n<p>A <b>funnel</b> counts distinct customers reaching each stage, usually via <code>.loc[mask, 'customer_id'].nunique()</code> per stage.</p>\n<p>Conversion rates divide one stage's distinct count by the previous stage's. Always guard the denominator (an <code>if denom else None</code>) to avoid a ZeroDivisionError.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Out of everyone who signed up, what fraction activated, and what fraction of those went on to pay?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>signups = events.loc[events.event_type == &#x27;signup&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()\nactivated = events.loc[events.event_type == &#x27;activated&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()\npaid = events.loc[events.event_type == &#x27;first_payment&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()\n\nresult = {\n    &#x27;signup_to_activation&#x27;: round(activated / signups, 4),\n    &#x27;activation_to_payment&#x27;: round(paid / activated, 4),\n}</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 60 signups, 50 activated, 37 paid &rarr; signup_to_activation = 0.8333, activation_to_payment = 0.74. Each rate is one stage's distinct customer count divided by the stage before it &mdash; the same funnel logic as the SQL version, just in pandas.</p>\n<p>Activation, engagement, retention, and monetization rates are the funnel metrics named directly in the role.</p>\n", "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)": "\n<p><code>scipy.stats.ttest_ind(a, b)</code> compares two group means; <code>scipy.stats.chi2_contingency(crosstab)</code> tests independence between two categorical variables.</p>\n<p>A confidence interval for a mean uses the normal approximation: <code>mean +/- 1.96 * (std / sqrt(n))</code> for a 95% interval.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Did the treatment group actually spend more per transaction than control?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>from scipy import stats\n\ntreat_ids = campaign.loc[campaign.exp_group == &#x27;treatment&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;]\nctrl_ids = campaign.loc[campaign.exp_group == &#x27;control&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;]\n\ntreat_amt = transactions[transactions.customer_id.isin(treat_ids)][&#x27;amount&#x27;]\nctrl_amt = transactions[transactions.customer_id.isin(ctrl_ids)][&#x27;amount&#x27;]\n\ntstat, pval = stats.ttest_ind(treat_amt, ctrl_amt)\nresult = {&#x27;treat_mean&#x27;: round(treat_amt.mean(), 2), &#x27;ctrl_mean&#x27;: round(ctrl_amt.mean(), 2), &#x27;p_value&#x27;: round(pval, 3)}</pre></div>\n<p>Result: treatment mean 184.91, control mean 228.18, p-value 0.321. That p-value is well above 0.05, so on this sample you can't reject the null &mdash; treatment doesn't look different from control, and it's actually control that's numerically higher, the opposite of what a naive read of \"treatment\" might assume.</p>\n<p>Both JDs for this role call out causal inference and experimentation explicitly &mdash; being fluent with these tests in plain Python (not just a library black box) is exactly what's being probed.</p>\n", "Simulation & probability": "\n<p><code>np.random.seed(n)</code> makes randomness reproducible &mdash; always seed before generating random data in an exercise, or your answer won't match anyone else's.</p>\n<p><code>np.random.randint</code>, <code>np.random.choice</code> (with a <code>p=</code> for biased draws) and <code>np.random.normal</code> cover most simulation needs; bootstrapping is just repeated <code>np.random.choice(..., replace=True)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Give me a 95% confidence interval on average transaction size without assuming a normal distribution.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>np.random.seed(0)\ndata = transactions[&#x27;amount&#x27;].values\nboot_means = [np.random.choice(data, size=len(data), replace=True).mean() for _ in range(1000)]\nresult = {&#x27;mean&#x27;: round(data.mean(), 2), &#x27;ci_low&#x27;: round(np.percentile(boot_means, 2.5), 2), &#x27;ci_high&#x27;: round(np.percentile(boot_means, 97.5), 2)}</pre></div>\n<p>Result: mean 207.00, with a bootstrap 95% CI of roughly (165.52, 252.84). Resampling the observed data with replacement 1000 times and taking the 2.5th/97.5th percentiles of the resample means sidesteps needing a closed-form formula, and the seed makes the answer reproducible.</p>\n<p>Monte Carlo estimation and bootstrap confidence intervals are a fast way to answer a probability question without deriving a closed-form formula under time pressure.</p>\n", "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions": "\n<p>List comprehensions (<code>[x for x in items if cond]</code>) and dict comprehensions (<code>{k: v for k, v in pairs}</code>) are the idiomatic, fast way to transform collections without an explicit loop.</p>\n<p><code>set()</code> dedups and supports <code>&amp;</code> (intersection) and <code>|</code> (union) directly; <code>collections.Counter</code> counts occurrences in one line.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Which high-spending customers ($1000+ lifetime) are also in the treatment group?\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>spend = transactions.groupby(&#x27;customer_id&#x27;)[&#x27;amount&#x27;].sum()\ntop_spenders = {cid for cid, total in spend.items() if total &gt; 1000}\ntreatment_set = set(campaign.loc[campaign.exp_group == &#x27;treatment&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;])\n\nresult = sorted(top_spenders &amp; treatment_set)</pre></div>\n<p>Result: [21, 40, 41, 45, 47, 58]. A set comprehension builds top_spenders in one line, and <code>&amp;</code> gives the intersection directly &mdash; no nested loop needed to answer \"which customers are in both lists.\"</p>\n<p>A HackerRank round will often test raw Python fluency separately from pandas &mdash; these are the fundamentals underneath every vectorized operation.</p>\n", "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic": "\n<p>Functions with clear inputs/outputs, sensible defaults, and guard clauses (checking for a zero denominator before dividing) are what separates working code from clean code.</p>\n<p><code>*args</code> collects extra positional arguments into a tuple; <code>**kwargs</code> collects extra keyword arguments into a dict &mdash; both let a function accept a flexible, unknown-in-advance number of inputs.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> \"Write a reusable function for a conversion rate that won't crash if the denominator is zero.\"</p>\n<div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>def conversion_rate(numerator, denominator):\n    if denominator == 0:\n        return None\n    return round(numerator / denominator, 4)\n\nsignups = events.loc[events.event_type == &#x27;signup&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()\nactivated = events.loc[events.event_type == &#x27;activated&#x27;, &#x27;customer_id&#x27;].nunique()\nresult = conversion_rate(activated, signups)</pre></div>\n<p>Result: 0.8333 (using activated=50, signups=60 from the funnel above). The guard clause on a zero denominator is the detail interviewers listen for &mdash; without it, an empty stage anywhere in a real funnel throws a ZeroDivisionError instead of a clean None.</p>\n<p>Being asked to \"walk through your thought process\" means the interviewer wants to see you structure logic clearly, not just produce a one-liner.</p>\n"};
const QUESTIONS = [{"id": 0, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "Before anything else, confirm the shape of the customers table we're working with.", "prompt": "Return the shape of customers as a (rows, columns) tuple.", "hint": "A DataFrame's .shape attribute is a tuple.", "solution": "result = customers.shape", "bonus": false}, {"id": 1, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "What columns do we actually have available on the customers table?", "prompt": "Return the column names of customers as a list.", "hint": "list(df.columns) gives you a plain Python list.", "solution": "result = list(customers.columns)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 2, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me a quick peek at the first five products so I know what we're working with.", "prompt": "Return the first 5 rows of products.", "hint": ".head(n) returns the first n rows.", "solution": "result = products.head(5)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 140, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that a DataFrame is pandas' core 2D labeled data structure - rows and columns, each column with its own dtype - and cover the standard inspection toolkit: .head()/.tail() for a quick peek, .shape for row/column counts, .dtypes for column types, .info() for a combined structural summary including non-null counts, and describe() for quick summary statistics on numeric columns. It should mention that selecting a single column with df['col'] returns a Series, while df[['col']] (double brackets) returns a single-column DataFrame - a subtle but common source of confusion."}, {"id": 3, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Pull up the customers based in the USA.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers where country is 'USA'.", "hint": "Boolean mask: df[df.country=='USA'].", "solution": "result = customers.loc[customers[\"country\"] == \"USA\", [\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 4, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Finance wants transactions in the 100 to 500 range.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for transactions with amount between 100 and 500 inclusive.", "hint": ".between(100, 500) is inclusive on both ends by default.", "solution": "result = transactions.loc[\n    transactions[\"amount\"].between(100, 500), [\"transaction_id\", \"amount\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 5, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Show Consumer-segment customers acquired through Paid or Referral channels.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for Consumer customers with acquisition_channel in ('Paid','Referral').", "hint": "Combine a mask with & and .isin(...).", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    (customers[\"segment\"] == \"Consumer\")\n    & (customers[\"acquisition_channel\"].isin([\"Paid\", \"Referral\"])),\n    [\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"],\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 141, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain boolean indexing: building a boolean Series from a condition (e.g. df['spend'] > 100) and using it inside df[...] to keep only matching rows, and that multiple conditions must be combined with & / | (not the plain and/or keywords) with each condition wrapped in parentheses. It should distinguish .loc (label/condition -based selection, inclusive of both endpoints when slicing) from .iloc (purely integer-position-based, exclusive of the end index like standard Python slicing), and mention .isin() as a clean way to filter against a list of values."}, {"id": 6, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "What are our 5 largest transactions, biggest first?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the 5 largest transactions, largest first.", "hint": "sort_values(..., ascending=False).head(n).", "solution": "result = transactions.sort_values(\"amount\", ascending=False)[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"amount\"]\n].head(5)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 7, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "Who were our 10 earliest-joining customers?", "prompt": "Return first_name and signup_date for the 10 earliest-signed-up customers, earliest first.", "hint": "Sort ascending on signup_date, break ties with customer_id for determinism.", "solution": "result = customers.sort_values([\"signup_date\", \"customer_id\"])[\n    [\"first_name\", \"signup_date\"]\n].head(10)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 8, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "Skip the top 5 transactions and show me the next 5 (ranks 6-10).", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the 6th through 10th largest transactions.", "hint": "Sort descending, then use iloc slicing to skip the first 5.", "solution": "result = transactions.sort_values(\n    [\"amount\", \"transaction_id\"], ascending=[False, False]\n)[[\"transaction_id\", \"amount\"]].iloc[5:10]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 142, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover sort_values() (with the column(s) to sort by, ascending=True/False, and that a list of columns sorts by the first with ties broken by the next), and the shortcut methods nlargest()/nsmallest() for getting the top or bottom N rows by a column without a full sort. It should mention that sort_values() returns a new DataFrame by default (not in-place) unless inplace=True is passed, and that resetting the index with reset_index(drop=True) is often needed after sorting if a clean, sequential index is required downstream."}, {"id": 9, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many transactions does each customer have?", "prompt": "Return a Series indexed by customer_id showing each customer's transaction count.", "hint": "groupby('customer_id').size().", "solution": "result = transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\").size()", "bonus": false}, {"id": 10, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "What's the total amount for each transaction status?", "prompt": "Return a Series indexed by status showing the summed amount per status, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "groupby('status')['amount'].sum().round(2).", "solution": "result = transactions.groupby(\"status\")[\"amount\"].sum().round(2)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 11, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Break down total revenue by product.", "prompt": "Return total (rounded) amount per product_id.", "hint": "groupby('product_id')['amount'].sum(), then round.", "solution": "result = transactions.groupby(\"product_id\")[\"amount\"].sum().round(2)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 143, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that groupby() splits a DataFrame into groups based on one or more column values, applies an aggregate function to each group independently, then combines the results - directly analogous to SQL's GROUP BY. It should cover common aggregates (.sum(), .mean(), .count(), .size()) and specifically the difference between .count() (counts non-null values per column within each group) and .size() (counts rows per group regardless of nulls). It should mention that the grouping column(s) become the index of the result unless as_index=False is passed."}, {"id": 12, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me customer counts split by both country and segment.", "prompt": "Return the number of customers per country and segment combination.", "hint": "groupby accepts a list of columns.", "solution": "result = customers.groupby([\"country\", \"segment\"]).size()", "bonus": false}, {"id": 13, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each customer, show the count and rounded total of their completed transactions.", "prompt": "Return txns (count) and revenue (rounded sum) per customer_id, completed transactions only.", "hint": "Filter first, then .agg(txns=('amount','count'), revenue=('amount','sum')).", "solution": "c = transactions[transactions[\"status\"] == \"completed\"]\nr = c.groupby(\"customer_id\").agg(txns=(\"amount\", \"count\"), revenue=(\"amount\", \"sum\"))\nr[\"revenue\"] = r[\"revenue\"].round(2)\nresult = r", "bonus": false}, {"id": 14, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Return product_id and txn_count only for products with more than 30 transactions.", "prompt": "Return product_id and txn_count for products with more than 30 transactions.", "hint": "Group, aggregate a count, then filter the resulting DataFrame like any other.", "solution": "g = (\n    transactions.groupby(\"product_id\")\n    .agg(txn_count=(\"transaction_id\", \"count\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\nresult = g[g[\"txn_count\"] > 30]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 144, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that groupby() accepts a list of columns to group by multiple keys at once, producing a MultiIndex result unless as_index=False is used. It should cover .agg() for applying different aggregate functions to different columns in a single call (e.g. {'spend': 'sum', 'orders': 'mean'}), including passing multiple named aggregations to the same column, and mention that custom aggregation functions (including lambdas) can be passed to .agg() when a built-in aggregate isn't enough."}, {"id": 15, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build a receipt-style view: transaction id, product name, and amount.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, product_name and amount by joining transactions to products.", "hint": "pd.merge or df.merge on the shared product_id key.", "solution": "result = transactions.merge(products, on=\"product_id\")[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"product_name\", \"amount\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 16, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me total revenue per product NAME rather than just its id.", "prompt": "Return total (rounded) amount per product_name.", "hint": "Merge first so you have a name to group by.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(products, on=\"product_id\")\nresult = m.groupby(\"product_name\")[\"amount\"].sum().round(2)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 17, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers have never made a single transaction?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers with no matching transactions.", "hint": "A left merge with indicator=True flags rows with no match on the right.", "solution": "m = customers.merge(\n    transactions[[\"customer_id\", \"transaction_id\"]],\n    on=\"customer_id\",\n    how=\"left\",\n    indicator=True,\n)\nresult = m[m[\"_merge\"] == \"left_only\"][[\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"]].drop_duplicates()", "bonus": false}, {"id": 145, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain pd.merge() as pandas' equivalent of a SQL join, covering the how parameter (inner, left, right, outer) and that on/left_on/right_on specify the matching key(s). It should mention that a mismatched or duplicated key on either side can silently multiply the row count in the result (a fan-out, the same risk as a SQL join), and that suffixes handles overlapping column names from both sides. It should also distinguish merge (matches on column values) from .join() (matches on index by default) and pd.concat() (stacks DataFrames together rather than matching on any key)."}, {"id": 18, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Are there any exact duplicate rows in the transactions table?", "prompt": "Return the number of exact duplicate rows in transactions.", "hint": ".duplicated() returns a boolean mask; sum it to count.", "solution": "result = int(transactions.duplicated().sum())", "bonus": false}, {"id": 19, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers have no signup_date recorded?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers where signup_date is missing (null).", "hint": ".isna() flags missing values.", "solution": "result = customers.loc[customers[\"signup_date\"].isna(), [\"customer_id\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 20, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a clean export, replace any missing acquisition_channel with the label 'Unknown'.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and acquisition_channel with nulls filled as 'Unknown'.", "hint": ".fillna('Unknown') replaces missing values in place of the original.", "solution": "result = customers[[\"customer_id\", \"acquisition_channel\"]].fillna(\n    {\"acquisition_channel\": \"Unknown\"}\n)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 146, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover detecting missing data with isna()/notna(), removing it with dropna() (including the subset and how='all' vs 'any' options), and filling it with fillna() (a constant, a forward/backward fill, or a computed value like the column mean). It should cover duplicated() for detecting duplicate rows and drop_duplicates() for removing them, including the subset parameter for defining duplicates based on only some columns, and the keep parameter for choosing which duplicate to retain."}, {"id": 21, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a mail merge, uppercase every customer's first name.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and an upper-cased first_name.", "hint": ".str.upper().", "solution": "result = customers.assign(first_name=customers[\"first_name\"].str.upper())[\n    [\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 22, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Extract just the year as a string from each transaction's txn_date using a regex, without converting to a real date type.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and yr: the 4-digit year pulled out of txn_date via a regex.", "hint": "str.extract(r'(\\d{4})').", "solution": "result = transactions.assign(yr=transactions[\"txn_date\"].str.extract(r\"(\\d{4})\"))[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"yr\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 23, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers have a first name that's exactly 4 letters long?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name where first_name has exactly 4 characters.", "hint": ".str.len() gives the length of each string.", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    customers[\"first_name\"].str.len() == 4, [\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 147, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that pandas' .str accessor applies string operations element-wise across a Series (e.g. .str.lower(), .str.contains(), .str.split()), avoiding the need for a manual Python loop. It should cover .str.extract() for pulling out a regex capture group into a new column, .str.contains() for boolean pattern matching (mentioning the na parameter for handling missing values safely), and .str.replace() for pattern-based substitution, including that a regex flag may need to be set explicitly depending on the pandas version."}, {"id": 24, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Tag every transaction with its calendar month, like '2024-05'.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and mth: the year-month of txn_date as text.", "hint": "pd.to_datetime(...).dt.strftime('%Y-%m') keeps it as text like the SQL version.", "solution": "result = transactions.assign(\n    mth=pd.to_datetime(transactions[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)[[\"transaction_id\", \"mth\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 25, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "Chart total revenue by calendar month, oldest first.", "prompt": "Return mth and revenue: total (rounded) amount per calendar month, ordered by month.", "hint": "Build the month column, group, sum, then sort the index.", "solution": "t = transactions.assign(\n    mth=pd.to_datetime(transactions[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\nresult = t.groupby(\"mth\")[\"amount\"].sum().round(2).sort_index()", "bonus": false}, {"id": 26, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For customers who transacted, how many days after signup was their first purchase?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and days_to_first: whole days from signup to first transaction.", "hint": "Convert both date columns with pd.to_datetime, take the min txn_date per customer, then subtract and use .dt.days.", "solution": "first_txn = (\n    transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"txn_date\"].min().reset_index(name=\"first_txn\")\n)\nm = customers.merge(first_txn, on=\"customer_id\")\nm[\"days_to_first\"] = (\n    pd.to_datetime(m[\"first_txn\"]) - pd.to_datetime(m[\"signup_date\"])\n).dt.days\nresult = m[[\"customer_id\", \"days_to_first\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 148, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain pd.to_datetime() for converting a column to proper datetime dtype, and the .dt accessor for extracting date parts (year, month, day, dayofweek) from a datetime Series, directly analogous to the .str accessor for strings. It should cover computing differences between two datetime columns (producing a Timedelta) and resampling or truncating dates to a coarser period (e.g. month-start) using dt.to_period() or floor/round-style operations, for grouping purposes."}, {"id": 27, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Flag every transaction as 'high' if amount is 200 or more, otherwise 'low'.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount and a size column ('high' if amount>=200 else 'low').", "hint": "np.where(condition, if_true, if_false) is the vectorized version of an if/else.", "solution": "result = transactions.assign(\n    size=np.where(transactions[\"amount\"] >= 200, \"high\", \"low\")\n)[[\"transaction_id\", \"amount\", \"size\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 28, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Tag every customer with a tier: 'top' for Enterprise, 'mid' for SMB, 'base' otherwise.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and tier using np.select with three segment-based conditions.", "hint": "np.select(conditions_list, choices_list, default=...) handles more than two branches.", "solution": "conds = [customers[\"segment\"] == \"Enterprise\", customers[\"segment\"] == \"SMB\"]\nchoices = [\"top\", \"mid\"]\nresult = customers.assign(tier=np.select(conds, choices, default=\"base\"))[\n    [\"customer_id\", \"tier\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 29, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Using .apply() with a custom function, classify each transaction's amount as 'small' (under 100), 'medium' (100 to 500), or 'large' (over 500).", "prompt": "Write a small function that returns the tier for a given amount, then apply it. Return transaction_id and tier.", "hint": "A plain Python function passed to Series.apply().", "solution": "def tier(a):\n    if a < 100:\n        return \"small\"\n    if a <= 500:\n        return \"medium\"\n    return \"large\"\n\n\nresult = transactions.assign(tier=transactions[\"amount\"].apply(tier))[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"tier\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 149, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that vectorized operations (direct arithmetic or comparison on whole columns at once) are almost always faster than .apply() with a row-wise lambda, since vectorized operations run in optimized C code rather than looping in Python. It should cover .map() for element-wise substitution using a dict or function on a Series, .apply() for more general row- or column-wise custom logic when a vectorized alternative doesn't exist, and np.where()/np.select() as vectorized alternatives to an if/elif chain - np.where for a single condition, np.select for multiple ordered conditions with corresponding choices."}, {"id": 30, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me a matrix: total transaction amount by product, broken out by status.", "prompt": "Return a pivot table of total (rounded) amount with product_id as rows and status as columns.", "hint": "pd.pivot_table(df, values=, index=, columns=, aggfunc='sum').", "solution": "result = pd.pivot_table(\n    transactions, values=\"amount\", index=\"product_id\", columns=\"status\", aggfunc=\"sum\"\n).round(2)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 31, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Cross-tabulate customer counts by country and segment.", "prompt": "Return a cross-tab of customer counts with country as rows, segment as columns.", "hint": "pd.crosstab(index_series, columns_series).", "solution": "result = pd.crosstab(customers[\"country\"], customers[\"segment\"])", "bonus": false}, {"id": 32, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Show average transaction amount by product and status in one table.", "prompt": "Return a pivot table of mean (rounded) amount, product_id as rows and status as columns.", "hint": "Same pivot_table call, aggfunc='mean' instead of 'sum'.", "solution": "result = pd.pivot_table(\n    transactions, values=\"amount\", index=\"product_id\", columns=\"status\", aggfunc=\"mean\"\n).round(2)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 150, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that pivot_table() reshapes long-format data into a wide grid, with one column's values becoming new column headers (columns=), another becoming the row index (index=), and a chosen aggregate function (aggfunc=, defaulting to mean) summarizing the values that land in each cell. It should distinguish this from pd.crosstab(), which specifically computes frequency counts (or another aggregate) between two or more categorical columns, and mention the fill_value parameter for handling cells with no matching data."}, {"id": 33, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Build a running total of transaction amounts ordered by date.", "prompt": "Return txn_date, amount and running_total: a cumulative sum ordered by txn_date then transaction_id.", "hint": "Sort first, then .cumsum() on the amount column.", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values([\"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"])\nresult = t.assign(running_total=t[\"amount\"].cumsum().round(2))[\n    [\"txn_date\", \"amount\", \"running_total\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 34, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Show a 3-transaction rolling average of amount, ordered by date, across all transactions.", "prompt": "Sort by txn_date (with transaction_id as a tiebreaker), then return txn_date, amount, and rolling_avg_3: a rolling mean over the last 3 rows, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "Series.rolling(3).mean() after sorting.", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values([\"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"])\nresult = t.assign(rolling_avg_3=t[\"amount\"].rolling(3).mean().round(2))[\n    [\"txn_date\", \"amount\", \"rolling_avg_3\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 35, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each transaction, show that customer's running average spend so far.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date, amount and running_avg per customer, ordered by txn_date.", "hint": "groupby(...).expanding().mean(), or cumsum()/cumcount() manually.", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values([\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"])\nt[\"running_avg\"] = (\n    t.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"]\n    .apply(lambda s: s.expanding().mean())\n    .reset_index(level=0, drop=True)\n    .round(2)\n)\nresult = t[[\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"amount\", \"running_avg\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 151, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover .rolling(window).agg() for computing a moving statistic (mean, sum) over a sliding window of rows, .cumsum()/.cumprod() for running totals, .shift() for accessing a prior (or, with a negative argument, a following) row's value within a group, and .diff() as a shortcut for the difference between a row and its shifted counterpart - directly analogous to SQL's LAG(). It should mention that these are almost always used together with groupby() when the calculation needs to reset independently for each group rather than running across the whole DataFrame."}, {"id": 36, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a cohort report, we need each customer's very first transaction date only.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_txn_date: each customer's earliest transaction date.", "hint": "groupby('customer_id')['txn_date'].min().", "solution": "result = (\n    transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"txn_date\"]\n    .min()\n    .reset_index(name=\"first_txn_date\")\n)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 37, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each customer, keep only their single most recent transaction.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date and amount for each customer's latest transaction (ties broken by highest transaction_id).", "hint": "Sort descending, then drop_duplicates(subset='customer_id', keep='first').", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values(\n    [\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"], ascending=[True, False, False]\n)\nresult = t.drop_duplicates(subset=\"customer_id\", keep=\"first\")[\n    [\"customer_id\", \"transaction_id\", \"txn_date\", \"amount\"]\n]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 38, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Rank customers by total spend, highest first, and give me their rank.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total_amount (rounded) and rnk: dense rank by total spend descending.", "hint": "Aggregate first, then .rank(method='dense', ascending=False) on the aggregate.", "solution": "s = (\n    transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index(name=\"total_amount\")\n)\ns[\"rnk\"] = s[\"total_amount\"].rank(method=\"dense\", ascending=False).astype(int)\nresult = s", "bonus": false}, {"id": 152, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover .rank() for computing a rank within a group (with the method parameter controlling how ties are handled, e.g. 'min', 'dense', 'first'), and the common 'latest record per group' pattern: sorting by the tiebreak column, then using groupby().head(1) or drop_duplicates(subset=group_cols, keep='last') to keep exactly one row per group - directly analogous to the SQL ROW_NUMBER()-plus-filter pattern."}, {"id": 39, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Turn our wide product-revenue-by-status pivot back into a tidy long format.", "prompt": "Build the product-by-status revenue pivot, then melt it back to columns product_id, status, revenue.", "hint": "pivot_table(...).reset_index() then pd.melt(..., id_vars='product_id').", "solution": "p = (\n    pd.pivot_table(\n        transactions,\n        values=\"amount\",\n        index=\"product_id\",\n        columns=\"status\",\n        aggfunc=\"sum\",\n    )\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index()\n)\nresult = p.melt(id_vars=\"product_id\", var_name=\"status\", value_name=\"revenue\")", "bonus": false}, {"id": 40, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Stack the country/segment customer-count cross-tab into a single tidy column.", "prompt": "Build a country x segment crosstab, then stack it into long form (country, segment, count).", "hint": ".stack() collapses the innermost column level into row-multiindex form; reset_index to flatten it.", "solution": "ct = pd.crosstab(customers[\"country\"], customers[\"segment\"])\nresult = ct.stack().reset_index(name=\"count\")", "bonus": false}, {"id": 41, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Unstack the country/segment counts back into the original wide matrix, to prove stack/unstack are inverses.", "prompt": "Build the stacked (long) country/segment counts, then unstack them back to a wide DataFrame.", "hint": ".unstack() is the inverse of .stack().", "solution": "ct = pd.crosstab(customers[\"country\"], customers[\"segment\"])\nlong = ct.stack()\nresult = long.unstack()", "bonus": false}, {"id": 153, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain the difference between wide format (each distinct measurement gets its own column) and long/tidy format (one row per single observation, with a column naming which variable it is). It should cover pd.melt() for converting wide data to long (specifying id_vars to keep fixed and value_vars to unpivot), and stack()/unstack() for pivoting a DataFrame's column index into/out of its row index. It should mention that most pandas groupby/aggregation operations expect long-format data, while wide format is often better for direct human reading or certain plotting needs."}, {"id": 42, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Tag every customer with the month they signed up in.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and cohort_month: signup month as text.", "hint": "pd.to_datetime(...).dt.strftime('%Y-%m').", "solution": "result = customers.assign(\n    cohort_month=pd.to_datetime(customers[\"signup_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)[[\"customer_id\", \"cohort_month\"]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 43, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "How big is each signup cohort?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and cohort_size: number of customers per signup-month cohort, ordered by month.", "hint": "Build the cohort_month column, then groupby().size().", "solution": "c = customers.assign(\n    cohort_month=pd.to_datetime(customers[\"signup_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\nresult = (\n    c.groupby(\"cohort_month\")\n    .size()\n    .reset_index(name=\"cohort_size\")\n    .sort_values(\"cohort_month\")\n)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 44, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "For each signup cohort, what fraction transacted again in the month right after signing up?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month, cohort_size, retained_m1 and retention_m1, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Compute each customer's cohort month and 'next month', then check membership against the set of (customer, active month) pairs.", "solution": "c = customers.copy()\nc[\"signup_dt\"] = pd.to_datetime(c[\"signup_date\"])\nc[\"cohort_month\"] = c[\"signup_dt\"].dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nc[\"next_month\"] = (c[\"signup_dt\"] + pd.offsets.MonthBegin(1)).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nt = transactions.copy()\nt[\"txn_month\"] = pd.to_datetime(t[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nactive_pairs = set(zip(t[\"customer_id\"], t[\"txn_month\"]))\nc[\"retained\"] = c.apply(\n    lambda r: (r[\"customer_id\"], r[\"next_month\"]) in active_pairs, axis=1\n)\ng = (\n    c.groupby(\"cohort_month\")\n    .agg(cohort_size=(\"customer_id\", \"size\"), retained_m1=(\"retained\", \"sum\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\ng[\"retention_m1\"] = (g[\"retained_m1\"] / g[\"cohort_size\"]).round(2)\nresult = g.sort_values(\"cohort_month\")", "bonus": false}, {"id": 154, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain the standard pandas cohort pattern: computing each customer's first-activity period (often via groupby().transform('min') on a date column), assigning that as their cohort label, then computing, for each cohort, what fraction of members were still active in each subsequent period relative to the cohort's original size. It should mention that the result is typically reshaped into a cohort-by-period grid (often via pivot_table) for a clean retention-curve view, directly mirroring the SQL cohort pattern but built from groupby/transform/pivot rather than window functions."}, {"id": 45, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many distinct customers reached each funnel stage?", "prompt": "Return event_type and customers: distinct customer count per funnel stage.", "hint": "groupby('event_type')['customer_id'].nunique().", "solution": "result = events.groupby(\"event_type\")[\"customer_id\"].nunique()", "bonus": false}, {"id": 46, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "In one row, give me distinct customers at every funnel stage.", "prompt": "Return a single-row summary with signups, activated, first_payment and repeat_payment counts.", "hint": "Build a dict of set sizes per stage, one key at a time.", "solution": "stages = [\"signup\", \"activated\", \"first_payment\", \"repeat_payment\"]\nresult = {\n    s: events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == s, \"customer_id\"].nunique() for s in stages\n}", "bonus": false}, {"id": 47, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Of everyone who signed up, what fraction ever activated? Protect against dividing by zero.", "prompt": "Return activation_rate: distinct activated customers divided by distinct signups, rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Compute both counts, then guard the denominator before dividing.", "solution": "signups = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"signup\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\nactivated = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"activated\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\nresult = round(activated / signups, 3) if signups else None", "bonus": false}, {"id": 155, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain computing, per entity (customer, session), whether each funnel step was reached (often via boolean flags per step), then aggregating those flags to get counts reaching each step, and dividing by a consistent denominator (usually the count reaching the first step) to get conversion rates. It should mention that step-over-step conversion (each step relative to the immediately preceding step) tells a different story from overall conversion (each step relative to the funnel's start), and that both are usually worth computing and reporting together."}, {"id": 48, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Before analyzing the experiment, sanity-check the group sizes for a sample ratio mismatch.", "prompt": "Return exp_group and n: customer count per experiment group.", "hint": "A simple groupby size, joined back to campaign.", "solution": "result = campaign.groupby(\"exp_group\").size().reset_index(name=\"n\")", "bonus": false}, {"id": 49, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Is the average transaction amount meaningfully different between treatment and control? Run a t-test.", "prompt": "Return t_stat and p_value (both rounded to 4 decimals) from an independent t-test of amount, treatment vs control.", "hint": "scipy.stats.ttest_ind(group_a_values, group_b_values).", "solution": "from scipy import stats\n\nm = transactions.merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")\na = m.loc[m[\"exp_group\"] == \"treatment\", \"amount\"]\nb = m.loc[m[\"exp_group\"] == \"control\", \"amount\"]\nt_stat, p_value = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=False)\nresult = {\"t_stat\": round(t_stat, 4), \"p_value\": round(p_value, 4)}", "bonus": false}, {"id": 50, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Is completion status independent of experiment group? Run a chi-square test of independence.", "prompt": "Return chi2 and p_value (rounded to 4 decimals) from a chi-square test of status vs exp_group.", "hint": "Build a crosstab first, then feed it into scipy.stats.chi2_contingency.", "solution": "from scipy import stats\n\nm = transactions.merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")\nct = pd.crosstab(m[\"status\"], m[\"exp_group\"])\nchi2, p_value, dof, expected = stats.chi2_contingency(ct)\nresult = {\"chi2\": round(chi2, 4), \"p_value\": round(p_value, 4)}", "bonus": false}, {"id": 156, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover the t-test for comparing means between two groups (e.g. average spend, treatment vs. control) and the chi-square test for comparing proportions/categorical distributions between groups (e.g. conversion rate). It should explain the p-value as the probability of seeing a difference this large (or larger) if there were truly no effect, and that a small p-value (below a chosen significance threshold, commonly 0.05) is evidence against the null hypothesis of no difference. It should mention confidence intervals as a range of plausible values for the true effect size, and that statistical significance alone doesn't guarantee the effect is large enough to matter practically."}, {"id": 51, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Simulate 1000 fair coin flips (seed 42) and tell me how many came up heads.", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(42), simulate 1000 flips of a fair coin (1=heads, 0=tails) and return the count of heads.", "hint": "np.random.seed(42) then np.random.randint(0, 2, size=1000).", "solution": "np.random.seed(42)\nflips = np.random.randint(0, 2, size=1000)\nresult = int(flips.sum())", "bonus": false}, {"id": 52, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Simulate rolling a fair six-sided die 10000 times (seed 7) and estimate the probability of rolling a 6.", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(7), simulate 10000 rolls of a die (values 1-6) and return the rounded (3 decimals) proportion that are 6.", "hint": "np.random.randint(1, 7, size=10000), then compare to 6 and take the mean.", "solution": "np.random.seed(7)\nrolls = np.random.randint(1, 7, size=10000)\nresult = round((rolls == 6).mean(), 3)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 53, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Bootstrap the mean transaction amount: resample with replacement 500 times (seed 1) and report the average of the resample means.", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(1), draw 500 bootstrap resamples (same size as the data) of transactions.amount and return the mean of the resample means, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "np.random.choice(array, size=len(array), replace=True) draws one resample; repeat 500 times.", "solution": "np.random.seed(1)\nvals = transactions[\"amount\"].values\nmeans = [\n    np.random.choice(vals, size=len(vals), replace=True).mean() for _ in range(500)\n]\nresult = round(float(np.mean(means)), 2)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 157, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain using numpy's random-sampling functions (e.g. np.random.choice, np.random.binomial, np.random.normal) to simulate a random process many times and empirically observe outcomes, rather than deriving them analytically - useful when an exact formula is hard to derive or verify by hand. It should mention setting a random seed for reproducibility, and the general Monte Carlo pattern: run the simulated process a large number of times, collect the outcome of each run, then summarize the distribution of outcomes (mean, percentiles) to estimate a probability or expected value."}, {"id": 54, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "From this list of transaction amounts, keep only the ones over 100.", "prompt": "Given amounts = [45.0, 120.5, 89.99, 300.0, 15.2], return a list of the amounts greater than 100.", "hint": "A list comprehension: [x for x in amounts if x > 100].", "solution": "amounts = [45.0, 120.5, 89.99, 300.0, 15.2]\nresult = [x for x in amounts if x > 100]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 55, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build a lookup dict from product_id to product_name without using pandas.", "prompt": "Given pairs = [(1,'Checkout'),(2,'P2P'),(3,'Credit')], return a dict mapping id to name.", "hint": "A dict comprehension: {k: v for k, v in pairs}.", "solution": "pairs = [(1, \"Checkout\"), (2, \"P2P\"), (3, \"Credit\")]\nresult = {k: v for k, v in pairs}", "bonus": false}, {"id": 56, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "From this list of countries (with repeats), give me just the unique ones.", "prompt": "Given countries = ['UK','USA','UK','Italy','USA','France'], return the distinct countries as a sorted list.", "hint": "set(countries) dedups; sorted() then gives a deterministic order.", "solution": "countries = [\"UK\", \"USA\", \"UK\", \"Italy\", \"USA\", \"France\"]\nresult = sorted(set(countries))", "bonus": false}, {"id": 158, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover the core built-in data structures and when each is appropriate: lists (ordered, allows duplicates, indexed by position), dicts (key-value pairs, fast lookup by key), and sets (unordered, unique elements only, fast membership testing). It should cover comprehension syntax for building each of these concisely from an iterable (e.g. [x for x in items if condition] for a list comprehension), and mention that a comprehension is generally both more readable and faster than the equivalent explicit for-loop-with-append pattern."}, {"id": 57, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Write a reusable function that classifies a transaction amount as 'high' or 'low' at a threshold of 200.", "prompt": "Define classify(amount) returning 'high' if amount>=200 else 'low'; return the results for [50, 250, 199.99, 200].", "hint": "A plain if/else inside a function, then call it in a list comprehension.", "solution": "def classify(amount):\n    return \"high\" if amount >= 200 else \"low\"\n\n\nresult = [classify(a) for a in [50, 250, 199.99, 200]]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 58, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Write a function that computes a safe division rate, returning None instead of crashing on a zero denominator.", "prompt": "Define safe_rate(numer, denom) that returns None if denom is 0, else round(numer/denom, 3). Test it on (10,0) and (10,4).", "hint": "Guard the denominator with an if before dividing.", "solution": "def safe_rate(numer, denom):\n    if denom == 0:\n        return None\n    return round(numer / denom, 3)\n\n\nresult = [safe_rate(10, 0), safe_rate(10, 4)]", "bonus": false}, {"id": 59, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "Write a function using *args that sums any number of transaction amounts passed to it.", "prompt": "Define total(*amounts) returning the sum, rounded to 2 decimals. Call it with (10.5, 20.25, 5).", "hint": "*args collects positional arguments into a tuple you can sum().", "solution": "def total(*amounts):\n    return round(sum(amounts), 2)\n\n\nresult = total(10.5, 20.25, 5)", "bonus": false}, {"id": 159, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover defining functions with def, including positional vs. keyword arguments and default parameter values, and standard control flow (if/elif/else, for and while loops). It should mention writing small, reusable functions to avoid repeating the same logic in multiple places, and cover common patterns like early returns to simplify conditional logic, and that mutable default arguments (e.g. a list or dict as a default parameter value) are a well-known Python pitfall worth knowing to avoid."}, {"id": 60, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "What data type is each column stored as in transactions?", "prompt": "Return the dtype of every column in transactions, as a Series.", "hint": ".dtypes returns a Series; cast to str for clean comparison.", "solution": "result = transactions.dtypes.astype(str)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 61, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Before we trust this data, check whether any columns in customers have missing values.", "prompt": "Return the count of missing values in each column of customers, as a Series.", "hint": "Chain .isna() and .sum().", "solution": "result = customers.isna().sum()", "bonus": true}, {"id": 62, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give finance the standard summary statistics for transaction amounts.", "prompt": "Return count, mean, std, min, quartiles and max for the amount column, as a DataFrame.", "hint": ".describe() on a single-column DataFrame (not a Series) keeps the result tabular.", "solution": "result = transactions[[\"amount\"]].describe()", "bonus": true}, {"id": 63, "topic": "DataFrame basics & inspection", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many distinct values does each column in customers actually take?", "prompt": "Return the number of unique values per column in customers, as a Series.", "hint": ".nunique() works column-wise on a DataFrame by default.", "solution": "result = customers.nunique()", "bonus": true}, {"id": 64, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We already reported on the USA and UK separately — show everyone else.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers NOT based in the USA or UK.", "hint": "~ negates a boolean mask; combine with .isin(...).", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    ~customers[\"country\"].isin([\"USA\", \"UK\"]), [\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 65, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which transactions fall outside our normal 100 to 500 review range?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for transactions NOT between 100 and 500.", "hint": "Negate a .between(...) mask with ~.", "solution": "result = transactions.loc[\n    ~transactions[\"amount\"].between(100, 500), [\"transaction_id\", \"amount\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 66, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "A support ticket asks for anyone whose name starts with 'J' or ends in 'a'.", "prompt": "Return first_name and country where first_name starts with 'J' or ends with 'a'.", "hint": "Use the .str accessor: .str.startswith('J') and .str.endswith('a').", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    customers[\"first_name\"].str.startswith(\"J\")\n    | customers[\"first_name\"].str.endswith(\"a\"),\n    [\"first_name\", \"country\"],\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 67, "topic": "Selecting, filtering, and boolean indexing", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Growth wants Paid signups specifically from Q1 2024.", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers with acquisition_channel 'Paid' and signup_date between 2024-01-01 and 2024-03-31.", "hint": "Text dates compare correctly with standard string comparison operators in YYYY-MM-DD form.", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    (customers[\"acquisition_channel\"] == \"Paid\")\n    & (customers[\"signup_date\"] >= \"2024-01-01\")\n    & (customers[\"signup_date\"] <= \"2024-03-31\"),\n    [\"customer_id\"],\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 68, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "What's the single smallest transaction we've ever processed?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the smallest transaction, breaking ties by lowest transaction_id.", "hint": "Sort ascending on both columns and take the first row.", "solution": "result = transactions.sort_values([\"amount\", \"transaction_id\"])[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"amount\"]\n].head(1)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 69, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "For a directory, list customers by country ascending, then name descending within each country.", "prompt": "Return first_name and country ordered by country ascending, then first_name descending.", "hint": "sort_values accepts a list of columns and a matching list of ascending flags.", "solution": "result = customers.sort_values([\"country\", \"first_name\"], ascending=[True, False])[\n    [\"first_name\", \"country\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 70, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Skip our newest signup and show me the next three most recent.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and signup_date for the 3 most recent signups, excluding the single most recent.", "hint": "Sort descending, then slice rows 1 through 3 (0-indexed).", "solution": "result = customers.sort_values(\n    [\"signup_date\", \"customer_id\"], ascending=[False, False]\n)[[\"customer_id\", \"first_name\", \"signup_date\"]].iloc[1:4]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 71, "topic": "Sorting and top-N selection", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "What was our second-biggest transaction of all time?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the second largest transaction only.", "hint": "Sort descending and use iloc[[1]] to grab just the second row.", "solution": "result = transactions.sort_values(\n    [\"amount\", \"transaction_id\"], ascending=[False, False]\n)[[\"transaction_id\", \"amount\"]].iloc[[1]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 72, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Does spend differ by acquisition channel? Join in the channel and average completed transactions.", "prompt": "Return the average completed transaction amount by the customer's acquisition_channel.", "hint": "Merge transactions to customers first, filter to completed, then groupby.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(\n    customers[[\"customer_id\", \"acquisition_channel\"]], on=\"customer_id\"\n)\nm = m[m[\"status\"] == \"completed\"]\nresult = m.groupby(\"acquisition_channel\")[\"amount\"].mean().round(2)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 73, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Ops wants a full breakdown: how many transactions, and how much revenue, per status.", "prompt": "Return count and total (rounded) amount per transaction status.", "hint": ".agg({'amount':['count','sum']}) computes multiple aggregates at once.", "solution": "g = transactions.groupby(\"status\")[\"amount\"].agg(count=\"count\", total_amount=\"sum\")\ng[\"total_amount\"] = g[\"total_amount\"].round(2)\nresult = g", "bonus": true}, {"id": 74, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a customer health view, show distinct product count and total transaction count per customer.", "prompt": "Return num_products (distinct product_id count) and num_txns (row count) per customer_id.", "hint": "agg(num_products=('product_id','nunique'), num_txns=('product_id','size')).", "solution": "result = transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\").agg(\n    num_products=(\"product_id\", \"nunique\"), num_txns=(\"product_id\", \"size\")\n)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 75, "topic": "GroupBy aggregation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Leadership wants signups broken down by year.", "prompt": "Return the count of customers grouped by signup year.", "hint": "Slice the first 4 characters of signup_date as the year, or use str[:4].", "solution": "result = customers.groupby(customers[\"signup_date\"].str[:4]).size()", "bonus": true}, {"id": 76, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers have more than 3 completed transactions?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers with more than 3 completed transactions.", "hint": "Filter to completed, group, count, then filter the aggregate result.", "solution": "c = transactions[transactions[\"status\"] == \"completed\"]\ng = c.groupby(\"customer_id\").size().reset_index(name=\"n\")\nresult = g[g[\"n\"] > 3][[\"customer_id\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 77, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which products average more than 150 per transaction?", "prompt": "Return product_id and avg_amount for products averaging more than 150 per transaction.", "hint": "Aggregate the mean, then filter on the resulting column.", "solution": "g = transactions.groupby(\"product_id\").agg(avg_amount=(\"amount\", \"mean\")).reset_index()\ng[\"avg_amount\"] = g[\"avg_amount\"].round(2)\nresult = g[g[\"avg_amount\"] > 150]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 78, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which countries already have 2 or more Enterprise customers?", "prompt": "Return country and enterprise_count for countries with 2+ Enterprise-segment customers.", "hint": "Filter to Enterprise first, then group and filter the count.", "solution": "e = customers[customers[\"segment\"] == \"Enterprise\"]\ng = e.groupby(\"country\").size().reset_index(name=\"enterprise_count\")\nresult = g[g[\"enterprise_count\"] >= 2]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 79, "topic": "Multi-column grouping & custom aggregations (.agg)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers have racked up more than 50 in refunds total?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers whose total refunded amount exceeds 50.", "hint": "Filter to refunded rows, group and sum, then filter that result.", "solution": "r = transactions[transactions[\"status\"] == \"refunded\"]\ng = r.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"].sum().reset_index(name=\"refunded\")\nresult = g[g[\"refunded\"] > 50][[\"customer_id\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 80, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build a full customer + product view for every completed transaction.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and product_name for every completed transaction (3-way join).", "hint": "Chain two .merge() calls: transactions->customers, then ->products.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(\n    customers[[\"customer_id\", \"first_name\"]], on=\"customer_id\"\n).merge(products[[\"product_id\", \"product_name\"]], on=\"product_id\")\nresult = m[m[\"status\"] == \"completed\"][[\"customer_id\", \"first_name\", \"product_name\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 81, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many distinct customers has each product actually reached?", "prompt": "Return product_name and customer_count (distinct customers who transacted in it).", "hint": "Merge to products, then groupby with nunique.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(products[[\"product_id\", \"product_name\"]], on=\"product_id\")\nresult = m.groupby(\"product_name\")[\"customer_id\"].nunique()", "bonus": true}, {"id": 82, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "The causal team needs every customer's name next to their treatment/control assignment.", "prompt": "Return first_name and exp_group for every customer, joining to the campaign table.", "hint": "A simple inner merge on customer_id.", "solution": "result = customers.merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")[[\"first_name\", \"exp_group\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 83, "topic": "Merging and joining DataFrames", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For an enterprise account review, which products have our Enterprise customers actually completed a purchase in?", "prompt": "Return first_name and product_name for Enterprise customers with a completed transaction.", "hint": "Filter customers to Enterprise before or after the joins, and filter transactions to completed.", "solution": "m = (\n    customers[customers[\"segment\"] == \"Enterprise\"]\n    .merge(transactions[transactions[\"status\"] == \"completed\"], on=\"customer_id\")\n    .merge(products, on=\"product_id\")\n)\nresult = m[[\"first_name\", \"product_name\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 84, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Some customer_id values may appear more than once by accident. Show only the first occurrence of each.", "prompt": "Return customers with duplicate customer_id rows removed, keeping the first occurrence.", "hint": ".drop_duplicates(subset=..., keep='first').", "solution": "result = customers.drop_duplicates(subset=[\"customer_id\"], keep=\"first\")", "bonus": true}, {"id": 85, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which transaction rows are complete duplicates of an earlier row (not the first appearance)?", "prompt": "Return the transaction_id values that are duplicate rows (excluding the first occurrence).", "hint": "keep='first' marks later duplicates as True in the mask.", "solution": "result = transactions.loc[transactions.duplicated(keep=\"first\"), [\"transaction_id\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 86, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For modelling, we need every customer's total spend, with non-transactors set to zero instead of missing.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and total_amount for every customer, with 0 where they have no transactions.", "hint": "Left-merge then fillna(0) on the aggregated column.", "solution": "g = (\n    transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index(name=\"total_amount\")\n)\nm = customers[[\"customer_id\"]].merge(g, on=\"customer_id\", how=\"left\")\nm[\"total_amount\"] = m[\"total_amount\"].fillna(0)\nresult = m", "bonus": true}, {"id": 87, "topic": "Handling missing and duplicate data", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Drop any row in customers that has at least one missing value, and tell me how many remain.", "prompt": "Return the number of customer rows remaining after dropping any row with a missing value.", "hint": ".dropna() removes rows containing any NaN by default.", "solution": "result = len(customers.dropna())", "bonus": true}, {"id": 88, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Tag every transaction status with whether it contains the letter combination 'ed' (a quick regex sanity check).", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, status and a has_ed boolean flag for whether status contains 'ed'.", "hint": ".str.contains('ed') returns a boolean Series directly.", "solution": "result = transactions.assign(has_ed=transactions[\"status\"].str.contains(\"ed\"))[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"status\", \"has_ed\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 89, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Extract just the year as a string from each signup_date using a regex, without converting to a real date type.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and yr: the 4-digit year extracted from signup_date via regex.", "hint": ".str.extract(r'(\\d{4})') pulls out the first capture group.", "solution": "result = customers.assign(yr=customers[\"signup_date\"].str.extract(r\"(\\d{4})\"))[\n    [\"customer_id\", \"yr\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 90, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Find customers whose first name starts with 'J' or ends with 'a', using a single regex this time.", "prompt": "Return first_name for names matching the regex pattern '^J|a$'.", "hint": ".str.match() or .str.contains(regex_pattern) both work with real regex.", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    customers[\"first_name\"].str.contains(r\"^J|a$\", regex=True), [\"first_name\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 91, "topic": "String manipulation and regex extraction", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build a slug-style identifier: lowercase first name with spaces replaced by underscores (none have spaces here, but make it robust).", "prompt": "Return customer_id and a slug column: first_name lowercased with spaces replaced by underscores.", "hint": ".str.lower().str.replace(' ', '_') chains cleanly.", "solution": "result = customers.assign(\n    slug=customers[\"first_name\"].str.lower().str.replace(\" \", \"_\")\n)[[\"customer_id\", \"slug\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 92, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Does signup day of week matter? Tag each customer with the numeric weekday they joined on.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and signup_weekday: the day of week (0=Monday) of signup_date.", "hint": ".dt.weekday gives 0 for Monday, matching Python convention (SQL's %w uses 0=Sunday, so expect a different numbering here).", "solution": "result = customers.assign(\n    signup_weekday=pd.to_datetime(customers[\"signup_date\"]).dt.weekday\n)[[\"customer_id\", \"signup_weekday\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 93, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For every transaction, how many days after that customer's signup did it happen?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and days_since_signup for every transaction.", "hint": "Merge to bring in signup_date, then subtract dates and take .dt.days.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(customers[[\"customer_id\", \"signup_date\"]], on=\"customer_id\")\nm[\"days_since_signup\"] = (\n    pd.to_datetime(m[\"txn_date\"]) - pd.to_datetime(m[\"signup_date\"])\n).dt.days\nresult = m[[\"transaction_id\", \"days_since_signup\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 94, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers joined during the second quarter of 2024?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers who signed up between 2024-04-01 and 2024-06-30.", "hint": "Text comparison works directly on YYYY-MM-DD strings, no conversion needed.", "solution": "result = customers.loc[\n    (customers[\"signup_date\"] >= \"2024-04-01\")\n    & (customers[\"signup_date\"] <= \"2024-06-30\"),\n    [\"customer_id\"],\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 95, "topic": "Date and time manipulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which months were unusually busy, with more than 15 transactions?", "prompt": "Return mth and txn_count for months with more than 15 transactions.", "hint": "Group by month, count, then filter the resulting DataFrame.", "solution": "t = transactions.assign(\n    mth=pd.to_datetime(transactions[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\ng = t.groupby(\"mth\").size().reset_index(name=\"txn_count\")\nresult = g[g[\"txn_count\"] > 15]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 96, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Ops wants a flag on any transaction that needs review: failed or refunded.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, status and a flag column: 'review' if failed/refunded, else 'ok'.", "hint": ".isin([...]) inside np.where works fine as the condition.", "solution": "result = transactions.assign(\n    flag=np.where(transactions[\"status\"].isin([\"failed\", \"refunded\"]), \"review\", \"ok\")\n)[[\"transaction_id\", \"status\", \"flag\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 97, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Map every country to its region: 'NA' for USA, otherwise 'Europe'.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and region (np.where on country=='USA').", "hint": "A single np.where handles a binary condition cleanly.", "solution": "result = customers.assign(\n    region=np.where(customers[\"country\"] == \"USA\", \"NA\", \"Europe\")\n)[[\"customer_id\", \"region\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 98, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give sales a priority ranking column: 1 for Enterprise, 2 for SMB, 3 for Consumer, sorted by priority.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, segment and priority, sorted by priority ascending.", "hint": "np.select for the mapping, then sort_values on the new column.", "solution": "conds = [customers[\"segment\"] == \"Enterprise\", customers[\"segment\"] == \"SMB\"]\nchoices = [1, 2]\nr = customers.assign(priority=np.select(conds, choices, default=3))\nresult = r.sort_values(\"priority\")[[\"customer_id\", \"segment\", \"priority\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 99, "topic": "Apply, map, and vectorized operations (np.where/np.select)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Using .apply(), build a combined label for each customer like 'Ugo - UK'.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and label built with .apply(lambda row: f\"{row.first_name} - {row.country}\").", "hint": "df.apply(func, axis=1) runs a function per row.", "solution": "result = customers.assign(\n    label=customers.apply(lambda r: f\"{r['first_name']} - {r['country']}\", axis=1)\n)[[\"customer_id\", \"label\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 100, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "The causal team wants a 2x2: average amount by experiment group and pre/post period.", "prompt": "Return a pivot table of mean (rounded) amount with exp_group as rows and period ('pre'/'post', split at 2024-04-01) as columns.", "hint": "Build the period column first with np.where, merge in exp_group, then pivot.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")\nm[\"period\"] = np.where(m[\"txn_date\"] < \"2024-04-01\", \"pre\", \"post\")\nresult = pd.pivot_table(\n    m, values=\"amount\", index=\"exp_group\", columns=\"period\", aggfunc=\"mean\"\n).round(2)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 101, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Cross-tabulate acquisition channel against segment as row percentages instead of raw counts.", "prompt": "Return a cross-tab of acquisition_channel (rows) vs segment (columns), normalized by row, rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "pd.crosstab(..., normalize='index').", "solution": "result = pd.crosstab(\n    customers[\"acquisition_channel\"], customers[\"segment\"], normalize=\"index\"\n).round(3)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 102, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build a pivot showing transaction counts by product and status, filling any empty combination with 0.", "prompt": "Return a pivot table of transaction counts (product_id rows, status columns), missing combos as 0.", "hint": "pivot_table's fill_value parameter handles this directly.", "solution": "result = pd.pivot_table(\n    transactions,\n    values=\"transaction_id\",\n    index=\"product_id\",\n    columns=\"status\",\n    aggfunc=\"count\",\n    fill_value=0,\n)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 103, "topic": "Pivot tables and cross-tabulation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me total spend per customer per product as a wide matrix.", "prompt": "Return a pivot table of total (rounded) amount, customer_id as rows, product_id as columns, 0 where absent.", "hint": "Same pattern as before with a different pair of index/columns.", "solution": "result = pd.pivot_table(\n    transactions,\n    values=\"amount\",\n    index=\"customer_id\",\n    columns=\"product_id\",\n    aggfunc=\"sum\",\n    fill_value=0,\n).round(2)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 104, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each transaction, show that customer's previous transaction amount.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date and prev_amount: the customer's previous transaction amount.", "hint": "Sort within customer, then .shift(1) on the amount column per group.", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values([\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"])\nt[\"prev_amount\"] = t.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"].shift(1)\nresult = t[[\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"prev_amount\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 105, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Did this customer spend more or less than last time? Compute the change.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date and amount_change: amount minus the previous transaction's amount, per customer.", "hint": ".diff() computes the difference from the previous row directly, per group.", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values([\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"])\nt[\"amount_change\"] = t.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"].diff().round(2)\nresult = t[[\"customer_id\", \"txn_date\", \"amount_change\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 106, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Alongside each month's revenue, show the prior month's revenue for comparison.", "prompt": "Return mth, revenue and prev_revenue: monthly revenue and the previous month's, ordered by month.", "hint": "Build the monthly series first, then .shift(1) on it.", "solution": "t = transactions.assign(\n    mth=pd.to_datetime(transactions[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\nm = (\n    t.groupby(\"mth\")[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index(name=\"revenue\")\n    .sort_values(\"mth\")\n)\nm[\"prev_revenue\"] = m[\"revenue\"].shift(1)\nresult = m", "bonus": true}, {"id": 107, "topic": "Window functions: rolling, cumulative sum, shift/diff", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Flag each transaction's amount versus the overall average, without collapsing any rows.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount and diff_from_avg: amount minus the overall average amount.", "hint": "Broadcast a scalar (the overall mean) against the whole column.", "solution": "avg = transactions[\"amount\"].mean()\nresult = transactions.assign(diff_from_avg=(transactions[\"amount\"] - avg).round(2))[\n    [\"transaction_id\", \"amount\", \"diff_from_avg\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 108, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Instead of latest-per-customer, give me latest-per-product for a freshness check.", "prompt": "Return product_id, transaction_id, txn_date and amount for each product's most recent transaction.", "hint": "Same drop_duplicates pattern, just partition by product_id instead.", "solution": "t = transactions.sort_values(\n    [\"product_id\", \"txn_date\", \"transaction_id\"], ascending=[True, False, False]\n)\nresult = t.drop_duplicates(subset=\"product_id\", keep=\"first\")[\n    [\"product_id\", \"transaction_id\", \"txn_date\", \"amount\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 109, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give leadership just the top 3 spenders, with their rank attached.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total_amount and rnk for just the top 3 spenders (rank via row_number-style ordering).", "hint": "Rank everyone first, then filter down to rnk<=3.", "solution": "s = (\n    transactions.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index(name=\"total_amount\")\n    .sort_values(\"total_amount\", ascending=False)\n)\ns[\"rnk\"] = range(1, len(s) + 1)\nresult = s[s[\"rnk\"] <= 3]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 110, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For regional coverage, find the single highest-spending customer in each country.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, country and total: the top spender per country.", "hint": "Merge in country, aggregate, sort within group, then drop_duplicates per country.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(customers[[\"customer_id\", \"country\"]], on=\"customer_id\")\ns = (\n    m.groupby([\"customer_id\", \"country\"])[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index(name=\"total\")\n)\ns = s.sort_values([\"country\", \"total\"], ascending=[True, False])\nresult = s.drop_duplicates(subset=\"country\", keep=\"first\")[\n    [\"customer_id\", \"country\", \"total\"]\n]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 111, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication (latest-record-per-group patterns)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Within each product, how does every transaction's amount rank against the others?", "prompt": "Return product_id, transaction_id, amount and rank_in_product: rank of amount within its product, highest first.", "hint": "groupby('product_id')['amount'].rank(ascending=False) ranks within each group directly.", "solution": "t = transactions.copy()\nt[\"rank_in_product\"] = (\n    t.groupby(\"product_id\")[\"amount\"].rank(method=\"min\", ascending=False).astype(int)\n)\nresult = t[[\"product_id\", \"transaction_id\", \"amount\", \"rank_in_product\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 112, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Reshape monthly revenue-by-product into one row per product with a column per month.", "prompt": "Return a pivot table of revenue with product_id as rows and month (YYYY-MM) as columns, 0 where missing.", "hint": "Build a month column first, then pivot_table with fill_value=0.", "solution": "t = transactions.assign(\n    mth=pd.to_datetime(transactions[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\nresult = pd.pivot_table(\n    t, values=\"amount\", index=\"product_id\", columns=\"mth\", aggfunc=\"sum\", fill_value=0\n).round(2)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 113, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Take that product-by-month wide table and melt it back into a tidy long table.", "prompt": "Build the product-by-month revenue pivot, then melt it to columns product_id, mth, revenue.", "hint": "reset_index() before melt so product_id becomes a regular column, not the index.", "solution": "t = transactions.assign(\n    mth=pd.to_datetime(transactions[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\np = (\n    pd.pivot_table(\n        t,\n        values=\"amount\",\n        index=\"product_id\",\n        columns=\"mth\",\n        aggfunc=\"sum\",\n        fill_value=0,\n    )\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index()\n)\nresult = p.melt(id_vars=\"product_id\", var_name=\"mth\", value_name=\"revenue\")", "bonus": true}, {"id": 114, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Combine two different breakdowns (by segment, by channel) into one tidy labelled table.", "prompt": "Return dim, value and revenue: revenue by segment stacked with revenue by acquisition_channel, each row labelled which dimension it's from.", "hint": "Build each breakdown separately with a constant 'dim' column, then pd.concat them.", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(customers, on=\"customer_id\")\nseg = (\n    m.groupby(\"segment\")[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index()\n    .rename(columns={\"segment\": \"value\"})\n)\nseg.insert(0, \"dim\", \"segment\")\nchan = (\n    m.groupby(\"acquisition_channel\")[\"amount\"]\n    .sum()\n    .round(2)\n    .reset_index()\n    .rename(columns={\"acquisition_channel\": \"value\"})\n)\nchan.insert(0, \"dim\", \"channel\")\nseg = seg.rename(columns={\"amount\": \"revenue\"})\nchan = chan.rename(columns={\"amount\": \"revenue\"})\nresult = pd.concat([seg, chan], ignore_index=True)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 115, "topic": "Reshaping data: melt, stack/unstack, wide vs long", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Reshape the campaign assignment into a one-hot style wide table: one column per group, 1/0 flags.", "prompt": "Return customer_id plus one column per exp_group value, containing 1 where that's their group and 0 otherwise.", "hint": "pd.get_dummies on the exp_group column, joined back to customer_id.", "solution": "d = pd.get_dummies(campaign[\"exp_group\"]).astype(int)\nresult = pd.concat([campaign[[\"customer_id\"]], d], axis=1)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 116, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Push retention one month further: what fraction of each cohort was still active two months after signup?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and m2_retention, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Same pattern as month-1 retention, but shift the comparison month forward by two instead of one.", "solution": "c = customers.copy()\nc[\"signup_dt\"] = pd.to_datetime(c[\"signup_date\"])\nc[\"cohort_month\"] = c[\"signup_dt\"].dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nc[\"m2\"] = (c[\"signup_dt\"] + pd.offsets.MonthBegin(2)).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nt = transactions.copy()\nt[\"txn_month\"] = pd.to_datetime(t[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nactive_pairs = set(zip(t[\"customer_id\"], t[\"txn_month\"]))\nc[\"retained\"] = c.apply(lambda r: (r[\"customer_id\"], r[\"m2\"]) in active_pairs, axis=1)\ng = (\n    c.groupby(\"cohort_month\")\n    .agg(size=(\"customer_id\", \"size\"), retained=(\"retained\", \"sum\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\ng[\"m2_retention\"] = (g[\"retained\"] / g[\"size\"]).round(2)\nresult = g[[\"cohort_month\", \"m2_retention\"]].sort_values(\"cohort_month\")", "bonus": true}, {"id": 117, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Forget transacting for a moment — what share of each cohort ever reached the activated stage?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and pct_activated, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Use the events table filtered to event_type=='activated' as your activity signal instead of transactions.", "solution": "c = customers.assign(\n    cohort_month=pd.to_datetime(customers[\"signup_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\nactivated_ids = set(events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"activated\", \"customer_id\"])\nc[\"is_activated\"] = c[\"customer_id\"].isin(activated_ids)\ng = (\n    c.groupby(\"cohort_month\")\n    .agg(size=(\"customer_id\", \"size\"), activated=(\"is_activated\", \"sum\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\ng[\"pct_activated\"] = (g[\"activated\"] / g[\"size\"]).round(2)\nresult = g[[\"cohort_month\", \"pct_activated\"]].sort_values(\"cohort_month\")", "bonus": true}, {"id": 118, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Of all our signup cohorts, which single one had the best month-1 retention?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and retention_m1 for the single cohort with the highest month-1 retention.", "hint": "Compute retention per cohort as before, then keep only the top row.", "solution": "c = customers.copy()\nc[\"signup_dt\"] = pd.to_datetime(c[\"signup_date\"])\nc[\"cohort_month\"] = c[\"signup_dt\"].dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nc[\"next_month\"] = (c[\"signup_dt\"] + pd.offsets.MonthBegin(1)).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nt = transactions.copy()\nt[\"txn_month\"] = pd.to_datetime(t[\"txn_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\nactive_pairs = set(zip(t[\"customer_id\"], t[\"txn_month\"]))\nc[\"retained\"] = c.apply(\n    lambda r: (r[\"customer_id\"], r[\"next_month\"]) in active_pairs, axis=1\n)\ng = (\n    c.groupby(\"cohort_month\")\n    .agg(size=(\"customer_id\", \"size\"), retained=(\"retained\", \"sum\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\ng[\"retention_m1\"] = (g[\"retained\"] / g[\"size\"]).round(2)\nresult = g.sort_values(\"retention_m1\", ascending=False)[\n    [\"cohort_month\", \"retention_m1\"]\n].head(1)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 119, "topic": "Cohort and retention analysis in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Beyond just returning, what share of each cohort became a genuine repeat payer?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and repeat_rate, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Same activation-rate pattern, checking for the repeat_payment event instead.", "solution": "c = customers.assign(\n    cohort_month=pd.to_datetime(customers[\"signup_date\"]).dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n)\nrepeat_ids = set(events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"repeat_payment\", \"customer_id\"])\nc[\"is_repeat\"] = c[\"customer_id\"].isin(repeat_ids)\ng = (\n    c.groupby(\"cohort_month\")\n    .agg(size=(\"customer_id\", \"size\"), repeat=(\"is_repeat\", \"sum\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\ng[\"repeat_rate\"] = (g[\"repeat\"] / g[\"size\"]).round(2)\nresult = g[[\"cohort_month\", \"repeat_rate\"]].sort_values(\"cohort_month\")", "bonus": true}, {"id": 120, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Of everyone activated, what fraction ever made a first payment?", "prompt": "Return activation_to_payment_rate rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Same pattern one stage further down the funnel.", "solution": "activated = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"activated\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\npaid = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"first_payment\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\nresult = round(paid / activated, 3) if activated else None", "bonus": true}, {"id": 121, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Of everyone who paid once, what share came back to pay again?", "prompt": "Return repeat_rate: distinct repeat_payment customers over distinct first_payment customers, rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Same ratio pattern, one stage further.", "solution": "paid = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"first_payment\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\nrepeat = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"repeat_payment\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\nresult = round(repeat / paid, 3) if paid else None", "bonus": true}, {"id": 122, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each stage after signup, how many signed-up customers never made it that far?", "prompt": "Return event_type and drop_off for activated, first_payment and repeat_payment.", "hint": "Subtract each stage's distinct count from the total distinct signups.", "solution": "signups = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == \"signup\", \"customer_id\"].nunique()\nrows = []\nfor s in [\"activated\", \"first_payment\", \"repeat_payment\"]:\n    n = events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == s, \"customer_id\"].nunique()\n    rows.append({\"event_type\": s, \"drop_off\": signups - n})\nresult = pd.DataFrame(rows)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 123, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math in pandas", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give leadership every stage-to-stage conversion rate in one summary row.", "prompt": "Return signup_to_activated, activated_to_first_payment and first_payment_to_repeat, each rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Compute each stage's distinct count once, then three ratios.", "solution": "counts = {\n    s: events.loc[events[\"event_type\"] == s, \"customer_id\"].nunique()\n    for s in [\"signup\", \"activated\", \"first_payment\", \"repeat_payment\"]\n}\nresult = {\n    \"signup_to_activated\": round(counts[\"activated\"] / counts[\"signup\"], 3),\n    \"activated_to_first_payment\": round(\n        counts[\"first_payment\"] / counts[\"activated\"], 3\n    ),\n    \"first_payment_to_repeat\": round(\n        counts[\"repeat_payment\"] / counts[\"first_payment\"], 3\n    ),\n}", "bonus": true}, {"id": 124, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give the causal team a 95% confidence interval around the treatment group's mean transaction amount.", "prompt": "Return mean, ci_low and ci_high (rounded to 2 decimals): a 95% CI for the treatment group's mean amount.", "hint": "Use the normal approximation: mean +/- 1.96 * standard error (std / sqrt(n)).", "solution": "m = transactions.merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")\nt = m.loc[m[\"exp_group\"] == \"treatment\", \"amount\"]\nmean = t.mean()\nse = t.std(ddof=1) / (len(t) ** 0.5)\nresult = {\n    \"mean\": round(mean, 2),\n    \"ci_low\": round(mean - 1.96 * se, 2),\n    \"ci_high\": round(mean + 1.96 * se, 2),\n}", "bonus": true}, {"id": 125, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Compute the completion rate for each experiment group as a quick lift check.", "prompt": "Return exp_group and completion_rate (rounded to 3 decimals): share of transactions with status completed.", "hint": "Build a boolean 'is_completed' column, merge in exp_group, then groupby mean.", "solution": "m = transactions.assign(\n    is_completed=(transactions[\"status\"] == \"completed\").astype(int)\n).merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")\nresult = m.groupby(\"exp_group\")[\"is_completed\"].mean().round(3)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 126, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Test whether the completion rate difference between groups is statistically significant, using a two-proportion z-test.", "prompt": "Return z_stat and p_value (rounded to 4 decimals) from a two-proportion z-test on completion rate, treatment vs control.", "hint": "statsmodels isn't available; compute manually: pooled proportion, standard error, then a z-score against the normal distribution.", "solution": "m = transactions.assign(\n    is_completed=(transactions[\"status\"] == \"completed\").astype(int)\n).merge(campaign, on=\"customer_id\")\na = m.loc[m[\"exp_group\"] == \"treatment\", \"is_completed\"]\nb = m.loc[m[\"exp_group\"] == \"control\", \"is_completed\"]\np1, p2 = a.mean(), b.mean()\nn1, n2 = len(a), len(b)\np_pool = (a.sum() + b.sum()) / (n1 + n2)\nse = (p_pool * (1 - p_pool) * (1 / n1 + 1 / n2)) ** 0.5\nz = (p1 - p2) / se\nfrom scipy import stats\n\np_value = 2 * (1 - stats.norm.cdf(abs(z)))\nresult = {\"z_stat\": round(z, 4), \"p_value\": round(p_value, 4)}", "bonus": true}, {"id": 127, "topic": "A/B testing & hypothesis testing (t-test, chi-square, confidence intervals)", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Compare average amount across all three transaction statuses at once with an ANOVA.", "prompt": "Return f_stat and p_value (rounded to 4 decimals) from a one-way ANOVA of amount across status groups.", "hint": "scipy.stats.f_oneway takes one array per group as separate arguments.", "solution": "from scipy import stats\n\ngroups = [g[\"amount\"].values for _, g in transactions.groupby(\"status\")]\nf_stat, p_value = stats.f_oneway(*groups)\nresult = {\"f_stat\": round(f_stat, 4), \"p_value\": round(p_value, 4)}", "bonus": true}, {"id": 128, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Simulate 200 draws (seed 3) from a normal distribution with the same mean and std as our transaction amounts, and report the simulated mean.", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(3), draw 200 samples from a normal distribution matching transactions.amount's mean/std, return the rounded (2 decimals) sample mean.", "hint": "np.random.normal(loc=mean, scale=std, size=200).", "solution": "mu, sigma = transactions[\"amount\"].mean(), transactions[\"amount\"].std()\nnp.random.seed(3)\nsample = np.random.normal(loc=mu, scale=sigma, size=200)\nresult = round(float(sample.mean()), 2)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 129, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Monte Carlo estimate: what's the probability that 2 randomly chosen dice (seed 5, 20000 trials) sum to 7?", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(5), simulate 20000 trials of rolling two dice and return the rounded (3 decimals) probability their sum is 7.", "hint": "Draw two arrays of rolls, add them elementwise, then compute the mean of the ==7 mask.", "solution": "np.random.seed(5)\nd1 = np.random.randint(1, 7, size=20000)\nd2 = np.random.randint(1, 7, size=20000)\nresult = round(float((d1 + d2 == 7).mean()), 3)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 130, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Simulate a biased coin (70% heads, seed 11, 5000 flips) and report the observed heads proportion.", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(11), simulate 5000 flips of a coin with P(heads)=0.7, return the rounded (3 decimals) observed proportion of heads.", "hint": "np.random.choice([1,0], size=5000, p=[0.7,0.3]).", "solution": "np.random.seed(11)\nflips = np.random.choice([1, 0], size=5000, p=[0.7, 0.3])\nresult = round(float(flips.mean()), 3)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 131, "topic": "Simulation & probability", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Randomly shuffle customers into two equal-ish groups (seed 21) as a mock A/A test, and report the group sizes.", "prompt": "Using np.random.seed(21), randomly permute customer_id and split into two halves; return the size of each half as group_a and group_b.", "hint": "np.random.permutation(array) shuffles; then slice the first half vs the rest.", "solution": "np.random.seed(21)\nids = customers[\"customer_id\"].values\nshuffled = np.random.permutation(ids)\nhalf = len(shuffled) // 2\nresult = {\"group_a\": int(len(shuffled[:half])), \"group_b\": int(len(shuffled[half:]))}", "bonus": true}, {"id": 132, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Flatten this list of per-customer transaction-id lists into one single list.", "prompt": "Given nested = [[1,2],[3],[4,5,6]], return a single flat list of all the values, in original order.", "hint": "A nested comprehension: [x for sub in nested for x in sub].", "solution": "nested = [[1, 2], [3], [4, 5, 6]]\nresult = [x for sub in nested for x in sub]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 133, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Count how many times each status appears in this list, without using pandas.", "prompt": "Given statuses = ['completed','failed','completed','completed','refunded','failed'], return a dict of status -> count.", "hint": "collections.Counter(statuses) does this directly, or build it manually with a dict.", "solution": "from collections import Counter\n\nstatuses = [\"completed\", \"failed\", \"completed\", \"completed\", \"refunded\", \"failed\"]\nresult = dict(Counter(statuses))", "bonus": true}, {"id": 134, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Invert this dict of customer_id to country, grouping customer ids by country.", "prompt": "Given cust_country = {1:'UK',2:'USA',3:'UK',4:'France'}, return a dict of country -> list of customer_ids.", "hint": "Loop through items and append to a dict of lists, using setdefault or a defaultdict.", "solution": "from collections import defaultdict\n\ncust_country = {1: \"UK\", 2: \"USA\", 3: \"UK\", 4: \"France\"}\ngrouped = defaultdict(list)\nfor cid, country in cust_country.items():\n    grouped[country].append(cid)\nresult = dict(grouped)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 135, "topic": "Core Python: lists, dicts, sets & comprehensions", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customer ids appear in BOTH of these two lists (e.g. transacted AND activated)?", "prompt": "Given a = [1,2,3,4,5] and b = [3,4,5,6,7], return the customer ids present in both, as a sorted list.", "hint": "Set intersection: set(a) & set(b).", "solution": "a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nb = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7]\nresult = sorted(set(a) & set(b))", "bonus": true}, {"id": 136, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Write a function with **kwargs that builds a labeled summary string from arbitrary named metrics.", "prompt": "Define summarize(**metrics) returning a dict with each value rounded to 2 decimals. Call it with revenue=123.456, txns=42.", "hint": "**kwargs collects keyword arguments into a dict you can iterate.", "solution": "def summarize(**metrics):\n    return {\n        k: (round(v, 2) if isinstance(v, (int, float)) else v)\n        for k, v in metrics.items()\n    }\n\n\nresult = summarize(revenue=123.456, txns=42)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 137, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Write a function that classifies a customer's segment priority, reusable across a whole list of segments.", "prompt": "Define priority(segment) returning 1 for Enterprise, 2 for SMB, 3 otherwise. Apply it to ['Enterprise','SMB','Consumer','Enterprise'].", "hint": "if/elif/else inside the function, then map() or a list comprehension over the list.", "solution": "def priority(segment):\n    if segment == \"Enterprise\":\n        return 1\n    elif segment == \"SMB\":\n        return 2\n    else:\n        return 3\n\n\nresult = [priority(s) for s in [\"Enterprise\", \"SMB\", \"Consumer\", \"Enterprise\"]]", "bonus": true}, {"id": 138, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Write a function that retries a flaky calculation up to 3 times, returning the first non-None result (simulate flakiness with a counter).", "prompt": "Define retry(fn, attempts=3) that calls fn() until it returns a non-None value or attempts run out, returning that value (or None). Test with a closure that returns None twice then 42.", "hint": "A while/for loop calling fn() and checking the return value each time.", "solution": "def retry(fn, attempts=3):\n    result = None\n    for _ in range(attempts):\n        result = fn()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    return result\n\n\ncalls = {\"n\": 0}\n\n\ndef flaky():\n    calls[\"n\"] += 1\n    return 42 if calls[\"n\"] >= 3 else None\n\n\nresult = retry(flaky, attempts=3)", "bonus": true}, {"id": 139, "topic": "Core Python: functions, control flow & writing reusable logic", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Write a generator function that yields running totals of a list of amounts, one at a time.", "prompt": "Define running_totals(amounts) as a generator yielding the cumulative sum after each element; return list(running_totals([10,20,5,15])).", "hint": "A generator uses yield inside a loop that tracks a running accumulator.", "solution": "def running_totals(amounts):\n    acc = 0\n    for a in amounts:\n        acc += a\n        yield acc\n\n\nresult = list(running_totals([10, 20, 5, 15]))", "bonus": true}]

let current=0;
const solved=new Set();
const flagged=new Set();
const savedQueries={};

function topicIndices(topic){const out=[];QUESTIONS.forEach((q,i)=>{if(q.topic===topic)out.push(i);});return out;}
function topicRequiredIndices(topic){return topicIndices(topic).filter(i=>!QUESTIONS[i].bonus&&!QUESTIONS[i].recall);}
function topicDone(topic){return topicRequiredIndices(topic).filter(i=>solved.has(QUESTIONS[i].id)).length;}
function requiredCount(){return QUESTIONS.filter(q=>!q.bonus&&!q.recall).length;}
function requiredSolvedCount(){let n=0;QUESTIONS.forEach(q=>{if(!q.bonus&&!q.recall&&solved.has(q.id))n++;});return n;}

function buildSidebar(){
  let html="";
  TOPICS.forEach((topic,ti)=>{
    const idxs=topicIndices(topic);
    const reqLen=topicRequiredIndices(topic).length;
    html+='<div class="topic" data-topic="'+ti+'">';
    html+='<div class="topic-head"><span class="tname">'+(ti+1)+'. '+topic+'</span><span class="frac" id="frac-'+ti+'">'+topicDone(topic)+'/'+reqLen+'</span><span class="topic-chev">hide</span></div>';
    html+='<div class="tbar"><i id="tbar-'+ti+'" style="width:'+(topicDone(topic)/reqLen*100)+'%"></i></div><div class="pad">';
    idxs.forEach(i=>{
      const q=QUESTIONS[i];
      const label=q.recall?"Recall: summarize this topic":(q.prompt.slice(0,40)+(q.prompt.length>40?"...":""));
      html+='<button class="qbtn'+(solved.has(q.id)?" done":"")+(q.bonus?" bonus":"")+(q.recall?" recall":"")+'" data-i="'+i+'"><span class="dot">'+(solved.has(q.id)?"\u2713":"")+'</span><span class="star">'+(flagged.has(q.id)?"\u2605":"")+'</span><span class="lbl">'+label+'</span></button>';
    });
    html+='</div></div>';
  });
  return html;
}
function buildMain(){
  return '<main>'
    +'<div class="qhead" id="qhead"></div>'
    +'<div class="readystrip"><div class="rt" id="rtTitle"></div><span class="rbar"><i id="rbar"></i></span><span class="rlabel" id="rlabel"></span></div>'
    +'<div class="lesson" id="lesson"><div class="lesson-head" id="lessonHead"><span class="lh" id="lessonTitle">Lesson</span><span class="chev" id="chev">hide</span></div><div class="lesson-body" id="lessonBody"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="ask" id="ask"><span class="albl">How it comes up in the interview</span><span class="atext" id="askText"></span></div>'
    +'<div class="promptbox collapsed" id="promptBox"><div class="prompt-head" id="promptHead"><span class="plbl">Write the code</span><span class="chev" id="promptChev">show</span></div><p class="prompt" id="prompt"><span id="promptText"></span></p><p class="hint" id="hint"></p></div>'
    +'<div class="editor-wrap">'
      +'<div class="editor-bar" id="pycalcBar"><span id="editorLabel">answer.py</span><button class="schema-toggle" id="schemaBtn">view tables</button></div>'
      +'<textarea id="sql" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocomplete="off"></textarea>'
      +'<textarea id="writtenAnswer" style="display:none" spellcheck="true" placeholder="Type your answer here - a few sentences covering the key ideas is plenty. Talk through it the way you would out loud in the interview."></textarea>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="actions"><button class="act run" id="runBtn">Run and check</button><button class="act run" id="checkAnswerBtn" style="display:none">Check answer</button><button class="act" id="solBtn">Show solution</button><button class="act" id="explainBtn">Explain solution</button><button class="act" id="explainWrongBtn" style="display:none">Explain my error</button><select id="explainModel" class="explain-model"><option value="sonnet">Sonnet 5</option><option value="haiku">Haiku 4.5</option></select><button class="act" id="resetBtn">Clear</button><button class="act" id="prevBtn">&larr; Previous</button><button class="act" id="nextBtn">Next &rarr;</button><span class="kbd">Cmd / Ctrl + Enter &middot; Shift + &larr;/&rarr;</span></div>'
    +'<div class="verdict" id="verdict"><span class="ic" id="vic"></span><span id="vmsg"></span></div>'
    +'<div class="stdoutbox" id="stdoutBox" style="display:none"><div class="lbl">Console output</div><pre id="stdoutText"></pre></div>'
    +'<div class="stdoutbox" id="varsBox" style="display:none"><div class="lbl">Your variables</div><div id="varsText"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="results" id="resultsBox" style="display:none"><h3>Your result</h3><div class="tablescroll" id="tableScroll"></div><div class="rowcount" id="rowCount"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="solution" id="solution"><div class="lbl" id="solutionLbl">One correct solution (there are often several)</div><pre id="solText"></pre><div id="solProse" style="display:none"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="explainbox" id="explainBox" style="display:none"><div class="lbl">Claude explains</div><div id="explainText"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="schema" id="schema"></div></main>';
}

function updateTopic(topic){
  const ti=TOPICS.indexOf(topic);const reqLen=topicRequiredIndices(topic).length;const done=topicDone(topic);
  const frac=document.getElementById("frac-"+ti);
  frac.textContent=done+"/"+reqLen;frac.className="frac"+(done===reqLen?" full":"");
  document.getElementById("tbar-"+ti).style.width=(done/reqLen*100)+"%";
  if(document.getElementById("rtTitle") && QUESTIONS[current].topic===topic){
    setReadystrip(topic,done,reqLen);
  }
}
function setReadystrip(topic,done,total){
  document.getElementById("rtTitle").innerHTML=topic+"<small>Topic "+(TOPICS.indexOf(topic)+1)+" of "+TOPICS.length+"</small>";
  document.getElementById("rbar").style.width=(done/total*100)+"%";
  const lab=document.getElementById("rlabel");
  if(done===0){lab.textContent="Not started";lab.className="rlabel";}
  else if(done<total){lab.textContent="In progress "+done+"/"+total;lab.className="rlabel prep";}
  else{lab.textContent="Ready \u2713";lab.className="rlabel ready";}
}
function updateProgress(){
  const done=requiredSolvedCount(),total=requiredCount();
  document.getElementById("doneCount").textContent=done;
  document.getElementById("progBar").style.width=Math.round(done/total*100)+"%";
  updateQuizGate(done,total);
}
function updateQuizGate(done,total){
  const btn=document.getElementById("quizBtn");
  if(!btn) return;
  if(done>=total){btn.classList.remove("locked");btn.removeAttribute("aria-disabled");}
  else{btn.classList.add("locked");btn.setAttribute("aria-disabled","true");}
}

function loadQuestion(i){
  current=i;const q=QUESTIONS[i];
  document.getElementById("qtick").textContent="Q"+(i+1);
  document.getElementById("qhead").innerHTML='<button class="star-toggle" id="starToggle" type="button" aria-label="Mark to come back to">☆</button><span class="pill '+q.diff+'">'+q.diff+'</span><span class="pill">'+q.topic+'</span><span class="pill est" id="estBadge">'+estimateLabel(q.diff)+'</span><span class="qtimer" id="qtimerMain">00:00</span><span class="cleared '+(solved.has(q.id)?"show":"")+'" id="clearedStamp">cleared</span>';
  renderStar(q.id);
  document.getElementById("starToggle").addEventListener("click",()=>toggleFlag(q.id));
  startQuestionTimer(q.diff);
  const lessonCard=document.getElementById("lesson");
  if(q.recall){
    lessonCard.style.display="none";
  }else{
    lessonCard.style.display="";
    document.getElementById("lessonBody").innerHTML=LESSONS[q.topic];
  }
  document.getElementById("askText").textContent=q.ask;
  document.getElementById("promptText").textContent=q.prompt||"";
  document.getElementById("promptBox").classList.add("collapsed");
  document.getElementById("promptChev").textContent="show";
  document.getElementById("hint").innerHTML="<b>Hint:</b> "+q.hint;
  document.getElementById("pycalcBar").style.display=q.recall?"none":"flex";
  document.getElementById("sql").style.display=q.recall?"none":"block";
  document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").style.display=q.recall?"block":"none";
  document.getElementById("runBtn").style.display=q.recall?"none":"";
  document.getElementById("checkAnswerBtn").style.display=q.recall?"":"none";
  document.getElementById("schemaBtn").style.display=q.recall?"none":"";
  if(q.recall) document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").value=savedQueries[q.id]||"";
  else document.getElementById("sql").value=savedQueries[q.id]||"";
  document.getElementById("verdict").className="verdict";
  document.getElementById("stdoutBox").style.display="none";
  document.getElementById("varsBox").style.display="none";
  document.getElementById("resultsBox").style.display="none";
  document.getElementById("solution").className="solution";
  document.getElementById("explainBox").style.display="none";
  setExplainWrongVisible(false);
  const solLbl=document.getElementById("solutionLbl"),solText=document.getElementById("solText"),solProse=document.getElementById("solProse");
  if(q.recall){
    solLbl.textContent="What a strong summary should cover";
    solText.style.display="none";
    solProse.style.display="block";solProse.innerHTML=renderMarkdown(q.modelAnswer);
  }else{
    solLbl.textContent="One correct solution (there are often several)";
    solText.style.display="block";solText.textContent=q.solution;
    solProse.style.display="none";
  }
  const reqLen=topicRequiredIndices(q.topic).length;setReadystrip(q.topic,topicDone(q.topic),reqLen);
  document.querySelectorAll(".qbtn").forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle("active",+b.dataset.i===i));
  document.getElementById("prevBtn").disabled=(i===0);
  document.getElementById("nextBtn").disabled=(i===QUESTIONS.length-1);
  document.querySelector("main").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"});
  if(q.recall) document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").focus({preventScroll:true});
  else document.getElementById("sql").focus({preventScroll:true});
}
function goToQuestion(delta){
  const next=current+delta;
  if(next>=0 && next<QUESTIONS.length) loadQuestion(next);
}
function markSolved(i){
  const id=QUESTIONS[i].id;
  if(!solved.has(id)){
    solved.add(id);
    const btn=document.querySelector('.qbtn[data-i="'+i+'"]');
    if(btn){btn.classList.add("done");btn.querySelector(".dot").textContent="\u2713";}
    const st=document.getElementById("clearedStamp");if(st)st.classList.add("show");
    updateProgress();updateTopic(QUESTIONS[i].topic);
  }
}
async function doCheckWritten(){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];
  const answer=document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").value.trim();
  if(!answer) return;
  savedQueries[q.id]=answer;
  const btn=document.getElementById("checkAnswerBtn");
  const orig=btn.textContent;
  btn.disabled=true;btn.textContent="Checking...";
  const box=document.getElementById("explainBox"),txt=document.getElementById("explainText");
  box.style.display="block";
  txt.innerHTML='<div class="explain-loading"><span class="spinner"></span>Reading through your answer…</div>';
  const verdict=document.getElementById("verdict"),vic=document.getElementById("vic"),vmsg=document.getElementById("vmsg");
  try{
    const payload={track:"python",mode:"trainer",questionId:q.id,kind:"grade",ask:q.ask,solution:q.modelAnswer,studentAnswer:answer,model:getExplainModel()};
    const r=await requestExplanation(payload);
    txt.innerHTML=renderMarkdown(r.content);
    box.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"nearest"});
    const strong=/\*\*strong\*\*/i.test(r.content) || /^strong/i.test(r.content.replace(/[*_]/g,""));
    verdict.className="verdict "+(strong?"ok":"no");
    vic.textContent=strong?"✓":"~";
    vmsg.textContent=strong?"Graded as strong - see feedback below.":"See feedback below for what to add.";
    logAttempt(q.id,answer,strong);
    if(strong) markSolved(current);
  }catch(e){
    txt.textContent=e.message;
  }finally{
    btn.disabled=false;btn.textContent=orig;
  }
}
async function doRunPycalc(){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];const code=document.getElementById("sql").value;
  const verdict=document.getElementById("verdict"),vic=document.getElementById("vic"),vmsg=document.getElementById("vmsg"),resultsBox=document.getElementById("resultsBox");
  if(!code.trim())return;
  const runBtn=document.getElementById("runBtn");
  runBtn.disabled=true;runBtn.textContent="Running...";
  let g;
  try{
    g=await runAndGrade(code, q.solution, q.ordered);
  }finally{
    runBtn.disabled=false;runBtn.textContent="Run and check";
  }
  const stdoutBox=document.getElementById("stdoutBox");
  if(g.stdout && g.stdout.trim()){
    stdoutBox.style.display="block";
    document.getElementById("stdoutText").textContent=g.stdout;
  }else{
    stdoutBox.style.display="none";
  }
  const varsBox=document.getElementById("varsBox");
  varsBox.style.display="none";
  if(g.error){
    verdict.className="verdict no";vic.textContent="!";vmsg.textContent="Python error: "+g.error;
    resultsBox.style.display="none";
    setExplainWrongVisible(true);
    return;
  }
  if(g.no_result){
    verdict.className="verdict no";vic.textContent="?";vmsg.textContent=g.msg;
    resultsBox.style.display="none";
    setExplainWrongVisible(true);
    if(g.vars && g.vars.length){
      varsBox.style.display="block";
      document.getElementById("varsText").innerHTML=g.vars.map(v=>
        '<div style="margin-bottom:6px"><b>'+v.name+'</b> <span style="color:var(--muted)">('+v.type+')</span><br>'+v.preview+'</div>'
      ).join("");
    }
    return;
  }
  resultsBox.style.display="block";
  document.getElementById("tableScroll").innerHTML=renderPyTable(g);
  const n=g.rowcount;
  document.getElementById("rowCount").textContent=n+" row"+(n===1?"":"s")+" returned"+(n>200?" (showing first 200)":"")+(g.source==="last_expression"?" (read from the last line of your code)":"");
  if(g.ok){verdict.className="verdict ok";vic.textContent="\u2713";vmsg.textContent=g.msg;markSolved(current);savedQueries[q.id]=code;}
  else{verdict.className="verdict no";vic.textContent="\u2717";vmsg.textContent=g.msg;}
  logAttempt(q.id,code,g.ok);
  setExplainWrongVisible(!g.ok);
}
function doRun(){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];
  if(q.recall) return doCheckWritten();
  return doRunPycalc();
}

let supabaseClient=null, currentUser=null, startIndex=0;

function initSupabase(){
  supabaseClient=window.supabase.createClient(window.__SUPABASE_URL__, window.__SUPABASE_ANON_KEY__);
}

function showSignOutBtn(){
  const btn=document.getElementById("signOutBtn");
  btn.classList.add("visible");
  btn.addEventListener("click",()=>{
    supabaseClient.auth.signOut().then(()=>window.location.reload());
  });
}

function requireAuth(){
  return supabaseClient.auth.getSession().then(({data:{session}})=>{
    if(session){currentUser=session.user;showSignOutBtn();return;}
    const gate=document.getElementById("authGate");
    gate.style.display="flex";
    return new Promise(resolve=>{
      document.getElementById("googleSignInBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>{
        supabaseClient.auth.signInWithOAuth({provider:"google",options:{redirectTo:window.location.origin}});
      });
      supabaseClient.auth.onAuthStateChange((event,session)=>{
        if(session && !currentUser){
          currentUser=session.user;
          gate.style.display="none";
          showSignOutBtn();
          resolve();
        }
      });
    });
  });
}

function hydrateProgress(){
  return supabaseClient.from("attempts").select("question_id,passed,query_text,created_at").eq("mode","trainer").eq("track","python").order("created_at",{ascending:true})
    .then(({data,error})=>{
      if(error||!data) return;
      data.forEach(r=>{
        const q=QUESTIONS.find(x=>x.id===r.question_id);
        if(r.passed)solved.add(r.question_id);
        if(r.passed||(q&&q.recall))savedQueries[r.question_id]=r.query_text;
      });
      if(data.length) startIndex=data[data.length-1].question_id;
    });
}

function hydrateFlags(){
  return fetchFlaggedIds("python","trainer").then(ids=>ids.forEach(id=>flagged.add(id)));
}

function toggleFlag(id){
  const on=!flagged.has(id);
  if(on) flagged.add(id); else flagged.delete(id);
  setQuestionFlag("python","trainer",id,on);
  renderStar(id);
  const btn=document.querySelector('.qbtn[data-i="'+current+'"]');
  if(btn){
    const st=btn.querySelector(".star");
    if(st) st.textContent=on?"★":"";
  }
  applySidebarFilters();
}

function renderStar(id){
  const btn=document.getElementById("starToggle");
  if(!btn) return;
  const on=flagged.has(id);
  btn.textContent=on?"★":"☆";
  btn.classList.toggle("on",on);
  btn.setAttribute("aria-label",on?"Marked to come back to":"Mark to come back to");
}

async function runExplain(kind){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];
  const btn=document.getElementById(kind==="solution"?"explainBtn":"explainWrongBtn");
  const box=document.getElementById("explainBox");
  const txt=document.getElementById("explainText");
  const orig=btn.textContent;
  btn.disabled=true;btn.textContent="Thinking...";
  box.style.display="block";
  txt.innerHTML='<div class="explain-loading"><span class="spinner"></span>Thinking through the solution…</div>';
  try{
    const payload={track:"python",mode:"trainer",questionId:q.id,kind,ask:q.ask,prompt:q.prompt||"",solution:q.recall?q.modelAnswer:q.solution,model:getExplainModel()};
    if(kind==="attempt")payload.userCode=q.recall?(savedQueries[q.id]||""):document.getElementById("sql").value;
    const r=await requestExplanation(payload);
    txt.innerHTML=renderMarkdown(r.content);
    box.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"nearest"});
  }catch(e){
    txt.textContent=e.message;
  }finally{
    btn.disabled=false;btn.textContent=orig;
  }
}

function logAttempt(questionId,queryText,passed){
  if(!supabaseClient||!currentUser) return;
  supabaseClient.from("attempts").insert({question_id:questionId,query_text:queryText,passed,mode:"trainer",track:"python"}).then(()=>{});
}

initSupabase();
initThemeToggle();
initTrainerSelect();

window.addEventListener("scroll",()=>{
  document.body.classList.toggle("scrolled",window.scrollY>30);
  syncHeaderHeight();
  setTimeout(syncHeaderHeight,200);
},{passive:true});

if(window.visualViewport){
  window.visualViewport.addEventListener("resize",()=>{
    document.body.classList.toggle("scrolled",window.scrollY>30);
    syncHeaderHeight();
  });
}

window.addEventListener("resize",syncHeaderHeight);
syncHeaderHeight();
setTimeout(syncHeaderHeight,300);

Promise.all([
  initPyodideEnv(),
  requireAuth().then(()=>Promise.all([hydrateProgress(),hydrateFlags()]))
])
.then(()=>{
  const app=document.getElementById("app");app.className="";
  app.innerHTML='<div class="wrap"><aside id="sidebar">'+buildSidebarFilterBar()+buildSidebar()+'</aside>'+buildMain()+'<div class="col-resizer" id="colResizer"></div><div class="right-col" id="rightCol"></div></div>';
  document.getElementById("schema").innerHTML=buildPySchema();
  document.getElementById("totalCount").textContent=requiredCount();
  document.querySelectorAll(".qbtn").forEach(b=>b.addEventListener("click",()=>loadQuestion(+b.dataset.i)));
  document.getElementById("runBtn").addEventListener("click",doRun);
  document.getElementById("checkAnswerBtn").addEventListener("click",doRun);
  document.getElementById("explainBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>runExplain("solution"));
  document.getElementById("explainWrongBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>runExplain("attempt"));
  initExplainModelSelect();
  initShellLayout();
  initTopicCollapse("collapsedTopicsPython");
  initSidebarFilters();
  document.getElementById("solBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>document.getElementById("solution").classList.toggle("show"));
  document.getElementById("resetBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>{
    const q=QUESTIONS[current];
    if(q.recall){document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").value="";document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").focus();}
    else{document.getElementById("sql").value="";document.getElementById("sql").focus();}
  });
  document.getElementById("schemaBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>document.getElementById("schema").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth"}));
  document.getElementById("prevBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>goToQuestion(-1));
  document.getElementById("nextBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>goToQuestion(1));
  const lh=document.getElementById("lessonHead");
  lh.addEventListener("click",()=>{const l=document.getElementById("lesson");l.classList.toggle("collapsed");document.getElementById("chev").textContent=l.classList.contains("collapsed")?"show":"hide";});
  const ph=document.getElementById("promptHead");
  ph.addEventListener("click",()=>{const p=document.getElementById("promptBox");p.classList.toggle("collapsed");document.getElementById("promptChev").textContent=p.classList.contains("collapsed")?"show":"hide";});
  const ta=document.getElementById("sql");
  ta.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{
    if((e.metaKey||e.ctrlKey)&&e.key==="Enter"){e.preventDefault();doRun();}
    if(e.key==="Tab"){e.preventDefault();const s=ta.selectionStart,en=ta.selectionEnd;ta.value=ta.value.slice(0,s)+"    "+ta.value.slice(en);ta.selectionStart=ta.selectionEnd=s+4;}
  });
  const wa=document.getElementById("writtenAnswer");
  wa.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{
    if((e.metaKey||e.ctrlKey)&&e.key==="Enter"){e.preventDefault();doRun();}
  });
  document.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{
    if(!e.shiftKey || document.activeElement===ta || document.activeElement===wa) return;
    if(e.key==="ArrowLeft"){e.preventDefault();goToQuestion(-1);}
    if(e.key==="ArrowRight"){e.preventDefault();goToQuestion(1);}
  });
  updateProgress();loadQuestion(startIndex);
})
.catch(err=>{document.getElementById("app").innerHTML='<div class="loading">Could not load the Python engine. Check your connection and reload. ('+err.message+')</div>';});
